ference_query_plan.md\nstrategy_validation_feedback.md\nreference_query_plan_prompt.md\npaper.fls\nreferences.pre_postdraft.bib\npaper_prep_20261113_180124.json\nREPRODUCIBILITY.md\npaper_diagnostics/postdraft_reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/postdraft_reference_query_plan.md\npaper_diagnostics/postdraft_reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/postdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\npaper_diagnostics/postdraft_reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/postdraft_reference_query_plan.json\npaper_diagnostics/postdraft_reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/postdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan.md\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan.json\nscientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json\nfatal: not a git repository (or any parent up to mount point /)\nStopping at filesystem boundary (GIT_DISCOVERY_ACROSS_FILESYSTEM not set).\n","exit_code":128,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat VENUE.md; cat /workspace/paper/.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; cat /workspace/paper/.codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat /workspace/paper/.codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat /workspace/paper/.codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat /workspace/paper/.codex/skills/generate-concept-figure/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat VENUE.md; cat /workspace/paper/.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; cat /workspace/paper/.codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat /workspace/paper/.codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat /workspace/paper/.codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat /workspace/paper/.codex/skills/generate-concept-figure/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"SomeKey2024\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n32. Run validator:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis validator also checks that `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` stay in sync, and traces every `@evidence[[...]]` link under `--paper-dir` against the `scientific-evidence/` bundle via the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier. Every link must classify as `resolved` or `anchored`; any `dangling` link is a hard error you must fix before finalization. If paper text carries `@evidence[[...]]` tokens but the bundle is missing, generate it first with `bash ../scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir .`.\n33. During repair passes, also rerun the full `validate-paper` workflow after each fix round so the next round starts from fresh feedback, not stale assumptions.\n34. When venue guidance is present, make the title, abstract, introduction, and conclusion explicitly reader-facing for that venue's scientific questions whenever the grounded evidence supports that framing.\n35. If `./paper_authors.tex` exists, treat it as the canonical author source. Keep `\\input{paper_authors.tex}` inside `\\author{...}` when possible; otherwise copy the exact file contents without edits.\n36. Keep the template's watermark block (`% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---` … `% --- end watermark ---`) in `paper.tex` unchanged; the orchestrator regenerates it before every host compile.\n\n# Outputs\n- `paper.tex`\n- `claims_grounding.json`\n- `figure_manifest.json` when figures are generated or required by strategy\n- Optional extra figures in `figures/` (grounded only, no cross-agent aggregate plots).\n---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n---\nname: generate-concept-figure\ndescription: Generate one reader-facing conceptual overview figure for paper generation as an editable draw.io diagram via the pinned Autoillustrate npm release, with a deterministic layout and an optional image-model restyle.\n---\n\n# Who runs this (important)\nDuring **paper generation the orchestrator owns this pipeline**: `src/scripts/paper.sh` and `paper_edit.sh` run the installed `autoillustrate` CLI synchronously as a post-draft step (`run_concept_figure_step`), derive every argument from `paper_strategy.json`, forward the selected outer agent, and promote the result to `./figures/<figure_id>.png`. The phase agents always run in a dedicated one-shot Docker container, even when the outer paper workflow is native; this limits them to the paper directory, selected-agent auth, and selected provider credentials. The paper-writing agent must **not** invoke this pipeline itself. The rest of this document describes the same CLI for standalone/manual use.\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate one reader-facing conceptual overview figure for the paper when a high-level glance-value visual will clarify the method, comparison story, or evidence structure. The figure is produced by the Autoillustrate pipeline (the `autoillustrate` npm release pinned in the agent image): each panel is a collision-checked, deterministically laid-out draw.io diagram, the panels are composed into a single editable `.drawio` file, and the composite is optionally restyled into a camera-ready PNG. The deliverable is a real `.drawio` source plus its export — only the final restyle pass touches a generative model.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper generation.\n- The paper needs an early conceptual figure that is easier to scan than a dense quantitative plot.\n- The grounded artifacts support a clean overview figure without inventing unsupported mechanisms or claims.\n- `paper_strategy.json` requests a required overview-style `diagram` figure or the paper prompt calls for an overview figure on page 1 or 2.\n- The figure is a methods/architecture/training-loop schematic with 1–3 structurally distinct panels. (For pure quantitative plots use matplotlib/seaborn instead.)\n\n# Prerequisites\nThe pipeline shells out to several tools. Confirm them before claiming a hard failure:\n- **`drawio` desktop CLI** on `PATH` (headless export). Without it Phases 2/4 cannot export.\n- **`autoillustrate` and `headless`** on `PATH`, plus the selected agent CLI (`claude`, `codex`, `cursor`, `gemini`, `opencode`, or `pi`).\n- For environment-authenticated OpenCode phase agents, set `PAPER_CONCEPT_FIGURE_AGENT_PROVIDER` to exactly one of `openai`, `gemini`, or `anthropic`; only that provider credential is forwarded.\n- **`OPENAI_API_KEY`** — the default VLM backend (`gpt-5.5`) for the Phase 3 per-panel inspection and the Phase 6 verification, and the image model (`gpt-image-2`) for the Phase 5 restyle. Without it those VLM calls and the restyle cannot run; you can still ship the deterministic Phase 4 export with `--skip-enhance`.\n- **`GEMINI_API_KEY`** — optional fallback. When OpenAI is unavailable, the paper orchestrator selects `nanobanana-pro`, `gemini-3.1-pro`, and `gemini-single` for enhancement and audits.\n- **A Python venv** with the toolkit deps. The container image bakes one and exposes its interpreter as `$VENV_PY`.\n\nBefore deciding a key is missing, check the active environment directly. OpenAI is the default; Gemini is the automatic fallback:\n```bash\npython - <<'PY'\nimport os\nfor k in (\"OPENAI_API_KEY\", \"GEMINI_API_KEY\"):\n    print(f\"{k}: {'available' if os.getenv(k) else 'missing'}\")\nPY\n```\nDo not claim a key is unavailable when the rendered paper prompt says it is expected to be available unless your direct check still shows it missing. Without an enhancement key, `--skip-enhance` still ships the deterministic Phase 4 export.\n\n# What to do\n1. Infer the central mechanism, comparison, or evidence story from grounded artifacts only. Decide whether one cohesive panel suffices or the content naturally splits into 2–3 structurally independent panels (e.g. *architecture · update rule · training loop*). Prefer one overview figure, not a full illustration suite.\n\n2. Confirm the installed CLI and selected agent are available:\n```bash\ncommand -v autoillustrate\nautoillustrate doctor --agent \"${AGENT:-claude}\"\n```\n\n3. In the project container, use the baked `$VENV_PY`. Native installations may let Autoillustrate create its cached venv or set `VENV_PY` explicitly.\n\n4. Run the pipeline against the manuscript. Resolve the strategy's exact figure id, pass `--output ./figures/<figure_id>.png`, forward the selected `--agent`, and provide a concise grounded prompt. Add `--skip-enhance` when no enhancement backend is available.\n\n**This pipeline can take ~1h** (it dispatches several selected-agent jobs and may run a VLM restyle). Run it **synchronously and block on it** — the caller must own the pipeline's full lifetime. Do **not** launch it as a background task that outlives the caller's turn (`run_in_background: true`, a trailing `&`, or a fire-and-forget agent step): when that turn ends the pipeline is orphaned and reaped mid-run, leaving the figure unrendered. In `paper.sh` and `paper_edit.sh` this is exactly what `run_concept_figure_step` does — a single synchronous invocation in the agent container, wrapped with `timeout` or Homebrew's `gtimeout` so a genuine hang is bounded. Native systems without either command skip this non-gating render step instead of launching an unbounded process. The pipeline writes `./figures/<figure_id>.png` (and `<figure_id>.prompts.json`) only on success; confirm those files exist before treating the figure as done.\n```bash\nautoillustrate ./paper.tex \\\n  --output ./figures/<figure_id>.png \\\n  --agent \"${AGENT:-claude}\" \\\n  --prompt \"Methods diagram that will be the main figure of this scientific research paper. <one or two sentences grounding the panels in the actual method>\"\n```\nThe orchestrator creates `runs/<YYYY-MM-DD-HHMMSS>/` in the working directory and writes the deliverables to `runs/<ts>/figures/`:\n- `full_figure.drawio` — editable draw.io source\n- `full_figure.png` — deterministic export (no model involved; always safe)\n- `full_figure_enhanced.png` — publication-grade restyle (only when Phase 5 ran)\n- `figure.json` — the validated 1–3 panel plan that drove the figure\n\nWith `--output` set, the pipeline also copies the best available figure (the enhanced export when Phase 5 ran, otherwise the deterministic export) to `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and drops the plan beside it as `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json`. The pipeline does not emit a PDF; the editable `full_figure.drawio` stays in the run workdir.\n\n5. Keep the visual style scientific and simple: clean diagrammatic composition, exact labels, no decorative clip-art, no unsupported architecture details, datasets, or numeric claims. When the paper contains multiple figures, keep colors logically consistent — reuse the same color for the same method/baseline/condition across figures.\n\n6. Confirm the promoted deliverable. With `--output` (step 4) the pipeline already wrote `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` — verify both exist. If you ran without `--output`, promote manually from the run workdir (enhanced export preferred, deterministic export as fallback):\n```bash\nRUN_FIGS=\"$(ls -td runs/*/figures | head -1)\"\nSRC_PNG=\"$RUN_FIGS/full_figure_enhanced.png\"; [[ -f \"$SRC_PNG\" ]] || SRC_PNG=\"$RUN_FIGS/full_figure.png\"\ncp \"$SRC_PNG\" \"./figures/<figure_id>.png\"\ncp \"$RUN_FIGS/figure.json\" \"./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json\"   # the prompt/plan log\n```\nThe editable `full_figure.drawio` stays in the run workdir so the figure can be re-edited later. The pipeline produces no PDF; the manifest does not require one for this figure.\n\n7. When the strategy or prompt asks for a required overview/`diagram` figure, do not silently replace this workflow with a hand-drawn or purely matplotlib/TikZ schematic. Use this skill first unless it is impossible.\n\n8. If a prerequisite is missing (for example, no `drawio` CLI) or the pipeline fails, log an explicit skip/failure reason in both `./memory.md` and `./global_memory/memory.md` before falling back to another figure implementation. Apply the same fallback logging rule if the generated figure is too poor to use.\n\n9. If you fall back to a non-drawio overview figure, record that fallback reason in the matching `figure_manifest.json` note so the run leaves a clear breadcrumb.\n\n10. If the final figure is used in `paper.tex`, add a matching `figure_manifest.json` entry that records `generator: generate-concept-figure`, `prompt_log_path` (the copied `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json`), and any `fallback_reason`. If the figure is not used, do not force it into the manuscript.\n\n# Outputs\n- `./figures/<figure_id>.png` (promoted from `full_figure_enhanced.png`, or `full_figure.png` when enhancement was skipped)\n- `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` (the run's `figure.json` plan, used as `prompt_log_path`)\n- Editable source preserved at `runs/<ts>/figures/full_figure.drawio`\n- Legacy explicit-path form remains valid: `./figures/concept_figure.png` and `./figures/concept_figure.prompts.json`\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat paper_strategy.md; cat draft_reference_gaps.md; cat postdraft_reference_query_plan.md; cat postdraft_references.bib; cat postdraft_references_provenance.json; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md | head -110; sed -n '1,170p' .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat paper_strategy.md; cat draft_reference_gaps.md; cat postdraft_reference_query_plan.md; cat postdraft_references.bib; cat postdraft_references_provenance.json; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md | head -110; sed -n '1,170p' .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Paper Strategy: A Bounded Evaluation of Few-Item Benchmark Prediction\n\n## Decision\n\n**Status: ready.** Use the `paper_standalone` family and the `write-paper-standalone` skill downstream. This stage supplies a strategy only, not a paper draft or final-paper validation.\n\n**Provisional title:** *Score-Targeted Item Selection for LLM Benchmark Prediction: Accuracy and Evaluation Limits*.\n\n**Central question:** What do grouped model holdouts reveal about fixed-prefix, source-only LLM benchmark prediction from 8, 16, or 32 responses, once measured prediction errors are distinguished from incomplete evaluations and failed controls?\n\n**Central thesis:** The score-covariance design with a group-tuned residual-profile/ridge predictor supports a conditional, heterogeneous description of few-item prediction on fixed benchmarks, but missing primary and baseline predictions and failed structural controls prevent a qualified overall accuracy or selection-advantage claim.\n\nThis is a method-and-limitations study, not a search diary, an overall superiority claim, or a claim that learned item selection necessarily worsens prediction. A completed unsuccessful qualification can support this bounded scientific story. Publication suitability does not change the method's qualification.\n\n## Evidence That Chooses the Story\n\nRead `paper_data.json.final_evaluation` independently of the absent `downstream_study`:\n\n- `final.qualification.status`: `completed`.\n- `final.qualification.outcome`: `not_qualified`.\n- `final.qualification.correct`: `false`.\n- `final.evidence.episodes`: three seeds, ten fixed benchmark universes, and budgets 8, 16, and 32, with target scores, predictions, completion indicators, structural checks, and comparison predictions.\n\nThe final evidence contains missing primary predictions for `commonsense` at seeds **5003** and **9001**, with the reference-baseline predictions absent in the same records. Several matched structural checks fail there as well. The evaluator assigns worst-case error defaults to missing outputs and missing baselines. Consequently, its reported MAE is not purely the average error of observed predictions. The same caveat applies to the reported aggregate RMSE and rank correlations.\n\n`task/evaluate_final.py` requires complete evaluations and passing structural checks; it does not require a novel-method accuracy improvement or target-adaptive acquisition. The observed omissions and structural-check failures identify concrete evidence-integrity limitations. They do **not** identify the underlying exception, implementation defect, or any unreported gate diagnosis. Preserve unknown causes as unknown.\n\nThe stored paired interval endpoints `[-1, 1]` are initialized fallback values when complete pairing is unavailable, not an informative measured confidence interval. The paired contrast also has its own missing-record convention: do not reconstruct it by subtracting the two reported aggregate MAEs.\n\n### Why This Beats the Alternatives\n\n| Candidate framing | Decision and grounded reason |\n| --- | --- |\n| Accuracy improvement supported by development success | Reject. Final qualification failed; development performance does not establish final validity or superiority. |\n| Uniform selection is better than learned selection | Reject as a causal thesis. The aggregate ordering mixes observed errors and missing-output penalties; the fitted uniform mode also refits/tunes its predictor. |\n| Broad method superiority across benchmarks or architectures | Reject. No evaluated architecture intervention, certified ancestry independence, or informative overall paired interval supports this. |\n| A protocol-only account with no estimator | Reject. The delivered source supports a concrete score-targeted design and prediction mechanism, and retained matched predictions support substantive descriptive comparisons. |\n| A bounded evaluation of the delivered estimator, its comparisons, and its evidence limits | Select. It uses both the scientific method and the final evidence without converting missing outputs into measured prediction failures. |\n\nNo `study.*` sources are available. Do not include search score differences, search-selection performance claims, or scored/unscored attempt counts using other source families instead. The frozen delivered method is the object of study; its discovery order is irrelevant.\n\n## Prior Story and Staged Inputs\n\nThis is not edit mode: there is no supplied edit-context file, seeded `previous_paper/`, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` bundle. The JSON field `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` rejects an inherited improvement narrative, **not** a nonexistent reviewed manuscript. Preserving or broadening an actual prior paper is inapplicable; recentering on the final evidence is the fresh framing decision.\n\nInspecting the delivered selected-method source and its `METHOD.md` establishes the method family directly. It does not establish which fitted instances selected a nonzero particle contribution. Final prediction evidence changes the story more strongly than the private memory's interpretation of the aggregate selection-control reversal.\n\nUse one shared scientific question with source-specific evidence blocks:\n\n- Open LLM Leaderboard-derived: `bbh`, `gpqa`, `musr`, `openllm_math`.\n- HELM-derived: `commonsense`, `gsm`, `legalbench`, `math`, `med_qa`, `mmlu`.\n\nThese are fixed staged binary-score universes, not interchangeable versions of every upstream benchmark and not a transfer or architecture intervention.\n\n## Method Groups and Explanation\n\n1. **Studied estimator:** source-only score-covariance item design and group-tuned residual-profile/ridge prediction. Explain the scalar target—the source model's full-benchmark mean—and why conditional score covariance is a plausible item-design objective. State its empirical, source-distribution character rather than promising target optimality.\n2. **Uniform selection with fitted prediction:** the recorded `no_diversity` condition replaces the order with seeded uniform prefixes and reruns fitting/tuning. This is a design-and-refitting intervention, not a selector swap holding numerical predictor parameters fixed.\n3. **Observed-mean controls:** `no_calibration` returns the mean of selected responses; the evaluator's `uniform` baseline returns a uniform-item sample mean. Neither is the fitted uniform-selection estimator. The former removes the learned prediction procedure, not just one calibration coefficient.\n4. **Anchor and IRT references:** retain the original anchor comparison wherever matched predictions exist, plus the evaluator's regularized two-parameter IRT reference. Explain their actual implementations; do not call them audited reproductions of published packages.\n5. **Supporting references and integrity checks:** source-mean prediction in a compact table; constant, copied, permuted and replayed observations in an integrity audit, not as candidate deployment methods.\n\nDiscarded residual-audit and earlier covariance-only branches need not occupy the narrative. Source-tuning options are not independent measured ablations. In particular, no final standalone residual-weight, offset, group-prior, or mixture intervention isolates the particle mechanism.\n\nThe method section should explain the actual source-only pipeline with equations and implementable detail:\n\n- Equal total source-group mass for acquisition and particle priors; conditional covariance/variance selection with a nugget and low-rank updates; one nested order, not target-adaptive queries.\n- Unweighted ridge fitting with intercept/shrinkage, with source-group-disjoint tuning and training-fold means. Do not describe every fitting operation as group-weighted.\n- Rasch-smoothed response profiles, residual interpolation, ability-offset particles, and a Bernoulli prefix likelihood with group-balanced prior mass.\n- Full-score reconstruction from the exact observed total plus expected unqueried total, then a convex blend with the linear fallback.\n- Rebuilding selection and prediction within source validation folds; tuning is not independent outer validation. Model posterior spread is not demonstrated calibrated uncertainty.\n\nThe delivered documentation supports these mechanisms. Retained scores do not prove that every component contributed to successful predictions.\n\n## Results: Separate Scored Aggregates from Measured Predictions\n\n### Main Evidence Order\n\n1. Establish the **availability and structural-check mask**, including missing primary/reference records and failed controls.\n2. Show a compact table of **recorded final aggregates**, explicitly labeled as including worst-case defaults where outputs are missing.\n3. Compare **available matched predictions** by benchmark, then examine budget and ranking behavior under the same stated support rule.\n4. Interpret heterogeneity and the evidence boundary, without turning conditional analyses into a repaired or qualified evaluation.\n\nThe aggregate table can include these recorded values:\n\n| Condition | Recorded aggregate MAE | Grounding source |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| Studied estimator | 0.08801465854359276 | `final.metric.mae` |\n| Uniform selection with fitted prediction | 0.044910877034389785 | `final.metric.no_diversity_mae` |\n| Selected-item observed mean | 0.10836174574059959 | `final.metric.no_calibration_mae` |\n| Uniform-item sample mean | 0.1335974367939037 | `final.metric.uniform_mae` |\n| IRT reference | 0.1186668212839868 | `final.metric.irt_mae` |\n| Anchor reference | 0.22047382735923254 | `final.metric.anchor_mae` |\n\nThese are **not** a pure measured-accuracy leaderboard. In particular, the lower fitted-uniform aggregate does not demonstrate a causal selection advantage. Missing reference outputs are not measured baseline MAE of one.\n\nFor conditional figures, first determine support from availability and the relevant structural flags, not from favorable metric values. Use the common case-seed intersection across each figure's displayed methods and state retained and missing support. Recompute budget-specific errors and rank correlations from stored predictions and `target_full_means`, with deterministic scripts. Keep missing predictions null and preserve every failed record in the audit.\n\nWhen conditionally aggregating, retain equal source-domain weight, equal cases within domain, equal retained seeds within case, and equal checkpoints when averaging budgets. Explain the changed within-case support normalization. Do not silently drop an entire failed case, silently replace the official aggregate, or represent this conditioning as confirmatory validation.\n\nShow all three seeds where available; use clearly labeled descriptive ranges, not invented error bars. Seeds and models overlap, and fixed benchmarks are not independent benchmark/item-population samples. An informative paired interval is unavailable. Any later dependence-aware uncertainty calculation must be separately labeled exploratory, derived from actual records, and never guessed here.\n\nThe main-text audit must preserve the failed qualification and describe incomplete records and failed checks. A successful checked replay (`final.metric.reset_max_abs_error = 0.0`) does not validate every other requirement. Unverified candidate diagnostics are not independent evidence.\n\n## Required Figures and Readability Budgets\n\n### Template Geometry\n\nThe inspected `paper.sty` specifies **one text column, 5.5 inches wide and 9 inches high**. Thus ordinary column width and full text width coincide. Use regular single-column figures at `\\linewidth`; a `full_width` strategy label does not call for a two-column float. Treat the supplied style as the selected format artifact, not proof that official-template freshness has been independently checked.\n\n### Figure 1 — Method and Evidence Overview\n\n- **Purpose:** immediate glance value for the source-only design, target prefix, estimator, and distinction between prediction error and unavailable evidence.\n- **Generator:** `generate-concept-figure`; this is the only planned figure using that workflow.\n- **Placement/footprint:** page 1 or 2; full text width, approximately **5.5 × 1.9 inches**, plus caption.\n- **Content budget:** one diagram, at most six concise blocks arranged in two rows with no more than three blocks per row; at most three visual streams; final-size text at least **9 pt**.\n- **Layout justification:** the mechanism is not legible from scores alone. Two short rows at the actual single-column width explain information access without dense architecture artwork. Separate the optional particle and ridge branches; do not draw arrows implying demonstrated improvement.\n\n### Figure 2 — Available-Pair Method Comparison\n\n- **Purpose:** measured MAE across benchmarks for primary, fitted uniform selection, anchor and IRT, without plotting missing-record defaults as observations.\n- **Generator/type:** `matplotlib`, grouped horizontal dot plots.\n- **Placement/footprint:** full text width, approximately **5.5 × 3.8 inches**, plus caption; two vertically stacked source-domain panels.\n- **Readability budget:** two panels, one per row; four and six benchmark categories respectively; four method series maximum; final-size text at least **8.5 pt**.\n- **Implementation:** equal-budget average within each available matched seed; seed points plus an explicitly descriptive available-seed mean; common-support annotations; unavailable markers outside the numerical axis. Use one legend and a shared labeled linear scale.\n- **Layout justification:** full width leaves room for case labels and four methods; vertical stacking avoids squeezing six cases into a narrow panel. If scale separation hides useful comparisons, simplify/rework the plot and use its accompanying absolute-value table—do not shrink labels or suppress the original anchor reference.\n\n### Figure 3 — Item Budget, Error, and Ranking\n\n- **Purpose:** compare fitted reconstruction and raw response means under selected and uniform items, using both error and ranking quality.\n- **Generator/type:** `matplotlib`, grouped dot plots without development trajectories.\n- **Placement/footprint:** full text width, approximately **5.5 × 2.5 inches**, plus caption; two side-by-side panels for MAE and Spearman correlation.\n- **Readability budget:** two panels in one row, three budget categories each, four method series maximum; final-size text at least **8.5 pt**; one shared legend.\n- **Methods:** primary, fitted uniform selection, selected-item observed mean, uniform-item sample mean. Use identical available support across the displayed methods and both panels, and document conditional aggregation weights.\n- **Layout justification:** three categories permit readable approximately 2.6-inch panels. Distinct metric labels and directions prevent readers from conflating lower error and higher correlation. Budgets are experimental conditions, not search milestones. Do not assume monotonic behavior.\n\n### Main Tables and Export Rules\n\nKeep the aggregate/default-disclosure table and compact missing-record/structural-check audit next to the results, not solely in the supplement. Quantitative figures must export **vector PDF and PNG at 300 DPI**, with LaTeX referencing the PDF whenever available. Keep source files and the editable overview. Raster-only full-width output must be regenerated at **at least 2400 px** wide; any later column-width raster-only output needs **at least 1200 px**. Never upscale existing rasters.\n\n## Body Layout: Substantive 8.5-Page Target\n\nICLR 2026 requires an anonymous **8–9-page body**, excluding references; an 8-page body qualifies for the layout requirement only when its final page is at least 75% full. Plan approximately:\n\n| Content | Body pages, including associated visuals |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Title, abstract, introduction, early overview | 1.4 |\n| Focused related work | 0.6 |\n| Problem, fixed-universe protocol, evidence mask | 1.3 |\n| Source-only design and prediction method | 1.7 |\n| Comparisons, budget/ranking results, aggregate and audit tables | 2.2 |\n| Discussion and limitations | 1.0 |\n| Conclusion | 0.3 |\n| **Target** | **8.5** |\n\nThese are planning allocations, not measured quantities. Spend available space on the estimator equations, comparator semantics, fixed-universe/grouped protocol, clear comparisons, uncertainty limits, and failure evidence. Do not pad with development branches, generic motivation, speculative experiments, or repeated caveats. Preserve enough main-text detail to understand the method and central limitations without the supplement.\n\nSuggested headings: **Introduction; Related Work; Fixed-Benchmark Score Prediction; Source-Only Item Design and Prediction; Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability; Prediction Error and Ranking on Available Pairs; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion.**\n\nOmit names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding statements, identifying links and internal host paths. Include the required separate **LLM Usage** section after references if convenient. Do not invent a conference checklist requirement; check applicable submission requirements downstream.\n\n## Supplementary Information After References\n\nThe supplement expands actual supplied evidence and reproducibility detail. It never hides qualification failure, substitutes for main comparisons, invents future results, or predicts later analysis rankings.\n\n| Heading | Grounded material and source IDs |\n| --- | --- |\n| Source-Only Design and Predictor Details | Exact design weights, nugget and low-rank updates, ridge/shrinkage grids, Rasch/residual/offset grids, group priors, likelihood and score decomposition, fold rebuilding and tie handling from the delivered `src/program.py` and `src/METHOD.md`. Existing evaluation anchor: `final.evidence.episodes`; link implementation paths separately, since episode evidence alone does not prove method details. |\n| Fixed Item Universes and Grouped Splits | Ten cases, staged matrix/audit checksums, source/target grouping, seeds and budgets, realized target support from `task/resources/final/manifest.json`, case metadata and `final.evidence.episodes`. Explain conservative ancestry heuristics, opaque source groups and supplied alignment attestations; do not claim newly audited raw outputs. |\n| Complete Case, Budget, and Seed Tables | All stored or deterministically derived MAE/RMSE/Spearman/Kendall results, reference predictions and support masks. Keep failed rows and recorded aggregates separate from available-pair calculations. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.mae`, `final.metric.rmse`, `final.metric.spearman`, `final.metric.kendall_tau_b`, and the reference/control `final.metric.*` entries listed in the JSON. |\n| Evaluation Completeness and Structural Checks | Exhaustive missingness and structural-check ledger; worst-case defaults; contrast/interval fallback; checked replay identity; unknown underlying failure cause. Sources: `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome`, `final.qualification.correct`, `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.control_failure_rate`, `final.metric.missing_episode_rate`, `final.metric.reset_max_abs_error`, `final.metric.delta_anchor_mae`, `final.metric.delta_anchor_ci_low`, `final.metric.delta_anchor_ci_high`. |\n| Reproducing the Delivered Evaluation and Figures | Delivered code and evaluator/manifest hashes, declared dependencies, staged data provenance, recorded execution configuration and artifact-to-visual transformations. Anchor: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome`; add actual local manifests/code links for implementation facts. Distinguish historical experiments from later plotting scripts, and measured timings from unverified runtime details. Do not silently repair the estimator and attribute new results to the frozen evaluation. |\n| LLM Usage | Precise evidenced assistance in ideation, implementation, literature planning, and manuscript planning/writing. Anchor the evaluated artifact with `final.evidence.episodes`, and use actual assistance provenance for disclosure—not as scientific metric evidence. Do not invent human verification or unrecorded API/model details. |\n\nThe final writer receives any authoritative appendix ranking separately. Route those analyses alongside this plan without retroactively guessing their rankings here.\n\n## Citation and Grounding Discipline\n\nPrivate grounding map:\n\n- Qualification facts: `final.qualification.*`, independently of any downstream-study status.\n- Final numerical summaries: `final.metric.*`.\n- Seeds, budgets, cases, targets, predictions, availability, lineage identifiers and structural checks: `final.evidence.episodes`.\n- Delivered method: `results/ideas/03_rasch_residual_profile_mixtures_on_fixed_score_targeted_prefixes/program_1/src/program.py` and its `src/METHOD.md`.\n- Interpretation of scoring, checks and fallback intervals: `task/evaluate_final.py`.\n- Staged provenance: `task/resources/final/manifest.json` and case metadata.\n\nTwo permitted exact-title entries were matched to reference provenance: `Vivek2023AnchorPB` and `Polo2024tinyBenchmarksEL`. They contextualize anchors and IRT, not exact baseline reproduction. Metadata retrieval is not proof of full-paper reading; only genuinely inspected permitted literature can ground substantive related-work statements. Do not pad the bibliography with unrelated retrievals. Do not identify/search for the prohibited unnamed paper, traverse citation graphs, or treat private memory as quantitative or citation evidence. Stop and record any accidental prohibited-source exposure without further investigation.\n\n## Language Guardrails and Downstream Handoff\n\nAvoid chronological headings: **Search Progression, Campaign Phases, Milestones, Timeline, First Valid Result, From Failure to Success, Experiment A / Experiment B, Iteration-by-Iteration Results, Winning Configuration**.\n\nAvoid diary phrases: **we then tried, subsequent improvements, next-best, later variants, first valid result, the search winner proves**.\n\nKeep internal labels out of visible scientific prose: **correctness rate, correct and eligible, eligible, combined_score, rubric, worker_01, worker_02**. No thresholded rate is necessary for this story; if one is later needed, define one reader-facing meaning and denominator explicitly.\n\nAlso reject unsupported claims: **statistically significant improvement, state-of-the-art accuracy, certified lineage generalization, calibrated posterior uncertainty, uniform sampling is superior, failed qualification means lower accuracy**.\n\nCarry failed qualification and its limitations through **writing, review, audit and revision**. Do not suppress them because other metrics look favorable. Distinguish observed missingness/structural failure from unknown underlying causes, and never equate unsuccessful qualification with demonstrated inferiority. Conditional plots remain conditional descriptions, not proof of valid overall performance.\n\nNo manuscript, claim-mapping JSON, new experiment, figure rendering, or final-paper validation belongs to this strategy stage. The downstream writer should implement auditable artifact transformations and the specified figures, preserve the frozen evidence and masks, then draft the standalone paper within these boundaries.\n# Draft Reference Gaps\n\n**Status:** ready\n\nTwo paragraph-specific gaps justify a small retrieval pass for Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark context and direct Rasch/IRT foundations. Existing related-work coverage needs no expansion. Both targets are medium priority; neither requires changing the paper's evidence-bounded thesis.\n\n## Grounded Review\n\nReviewed `paper.tex`, all 25 entries in `references.bib` and their existing provenance, the 30 claims in `claims_grounding.json`, the structure and final-evaluation evidence in `paper_data.json`, `paper_strategy.json`, and `global_memory_summary.md`. All manuscript citation keys resolve, and every existing bibliography entry is cited. Global memory was used only as qualitative context, not numerical evidence. No references were retrieved.\n\n## 1. Benchmark Collection Context\n\n- **Target ID:** `evaluation-leaderboard-context`\n- **Section:** Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability\n- **Paragraph:** Fixed Benchmarks and Grouped Model Holdouts, opening paragraph.\n- **Priority / intent:** medium / benchmark_context\n- **Anchor:** “four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math)”\n- **Gap:** The paragraph identifies a named upstream collection without a direct collection reference; no dedicated Leaderboard source appears in the bibliography. HELM is already represented by `Liang2023`, which can be reused here without new retrieval. `Wang2024` concerns MMLU-Pro and must not be repurposed as provenance for the staged MMLU case.\n- **Focused search topic:** Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark collection documentation covering BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math.\n- **Bounded intervention:** Add one authoritative collection citation. Retain the staged names and the explicit warning against assuming public-benchmark identity. Case counts, fixed item universes, snapshot identity, and filtering remain supported by the supplied artifacts, not by this citation. Do not commission separate searches for all ten benchmarks.\n\n## 2. Named Item-Response Model Foundations\n\n- **Target ID:** `method-rasch-irt-foundations`\n- **Section:** Method\n- **Paragraph:** Linear and Residual-Profile Reconstruction, introduction of Rasch smoothing.\n- **Priority / intent:** medium / background\n- **Anchor:** “The nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model.”\n- **Gap:** The named response family lacks a direct foundational citation. `Andersen1970` is cited in Related Work for an estimation procedure explicitly not used here, and its stored abstract addresses conditional estimation. Broad statistical texts and the already cited LLM compression methods do not directly anchor the Rasch/2PL distinction in these paragraphs. The local IRT comparator in Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability also introduces two-parameter logistic responses and fitted item information without a direct foundation citation.\n- **Focused search topics:** Rasch logistic ability–difficulty model foundations; two-parameter logistic discrimination and Fisher item information.\n- **Bounded intervention:** Retrieve one authoritative source covering both families, or at most two complementary sources. Cite the Rasch introduction and reuse the relevant source at the local IRT comparator if supported. Preserve the fixed-ability, penalized custom-fit description and the disclaimer that local references are not official reproductions. No claim of profile-component benefit or target-calibrated uncertainty should be added.\n\n## Coverage That Does Not Justify Retrieval\n\n- Related Work directly cites Anchor Points, tinyBenchmarks, metabench, reference-instance prediction, and active evaluation acquisition. There is no missing comparator-family paragraph to expand.\n- Scalar-target design, ridge regression, validation dependence, domain shift, missingness, ranking statistics, and bootstrap background already have relevant citations. The selector is explicitly a heuristic, not an unsupported optimality theorem.\n- Numerical accuracy, availability masks, failed qualification, structural diagnostics, and local implementation details are grounded in the supplied evidence. More citations cannot repair missing predictions, isolate a profile contribution, or establish overall superiority.\n- Do not add literature on deployment speedups, new benchmark populations, target-adaptive policies, or calibrated stopping rules: those would expand rather than support the existing story.\n\n## Handoff\n\nRetrieve approximately two to three sources total across these two targets, preferring reuse when one IRT source covers both model families. This memo does not prescribe new paper sections or a broad rewrite. The manuscript, bibliography, and reference provenance remain unchanged.\n# Post-Draft Reference Query Plan\n\n**Status:** ready  \n**Scope:** Three focused query specifications for the two medium-priority gaps in `draft_reference_gaps.md` and `draft_reference_gaps.json`. Query 3 is conditional. A later retrieval pass should add approximately two to three unique sources, not refresh the bibliography. No references were retrieved in this stage.\n\n## Central Topic\n\nEvidence-bounded prediction of LLM full binary benchmark scores and rankings from fixed 8-, 16-, or 32-item prefixes using source-only score-covariance selection and ridge/Rasch-residual reconstruction. Only upstream Open LLM Leaderboard collection context and direct Rasch/2PL foundations need incremental citation support.\n\n## Grounding and Existing Coverage\n\nThe gap memo and JSON were read first and drive every query below. The manuscript, all 25 existing bibliography entries and their stored provenance, the original query-plan schema, and the global memory summary were checked locally. Global memory supplies qualitative context only, not numerical evidence.\n\n- **Core methods:** Fixed source-only prefixes, grouped tuning, and ridge prediction already have adequate methodological context. The Rasch introduction at `paper.tex:86` needs direct model-family support; its fixed-ability, group-weighted penalized fit and optional profile blend remain custom, artifact-grounded details.\n- **Comparators:** The local 2PL comparator at `paper.tex:130` and its information formula at `paper.tex:293` need foundational support. Anchor Points, tinyBenchmarks, metabench, reference-instance prediction, and active evaluation acquisition already have direct citations; do not expand their coverage.\n- **Benchmarks:** One collection reference is missing for the four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases named at `paper.tex:115`. For the six HELM-derived cases in that paragraph, reuse `Liang2023` without retrieval. `Wang2024` addresses MMLU-Pro and is not provenance for staged MMLU.\n- **Architectural shifts:** None are requested or justified.\n- **Result-driven boundaries:** Missing predictions, failed qualification, structural diagnostics, and retained-support results remain artifact-grounded. Citations cannot repair those outcomes, isolate a profile contribution, establish selector superiority, or calibrate target uncertainty.\n\nThe original plan already included a Leaderboard collection query, but no dedicated collection source is present in the resulting bibliography or provenance. Reopening that specific unresolved gap is warranted; rerunning successful comparator searches is not. The stored abstract for `Andersen1970` concerns conditional maximum-likelihood estimation, explicitly distinguished from this implementation, rather than directly supporting both response-model families.\n\n## Queries\n\n### 1. Upstream Leaderboard Collection Context\n\n- **Query:** `Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark collection documentation BBH GPQA MuSR OpenLLM Math`\n- **Category:** `benchmark_context`\n- **Gap / location:** `evaluation-leaderboard-context`; Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability / Fixed Benchmarks and Grouped Model Holdouts, opening paragraph, `paper.tex:115`.\n- **Anchor:** “four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math)”\n- **Rationale:** Seek one authoritative collection source documenting the upstream benchmark composition, closing the unresolved collection citation gap. Do not use it to substantiate staged counts, filtering, exact snapshot identity, or equivalence with public benchmark versions. Reuse existing HELM support instead of searching again; do not substitute MMLU-Pro provenance for staged MMLU.\n- **Completion condition:** One verified collection source supports the named upstream context. Preserve staged case names and the manuscript's explicit non-equivalence warning; do not commission separate searches for every benchmark.\n\n### 2. Rasch Response-Model Foundations\n\n- **Query:** `Rasch logistic item-response model ability item difficulty foundational reference`\n- **Category:** `core_method`\n- **Gap / location:** `method-rasch-irt-foundations`; Method / Linear and Residual-Profile Reconstruction, `paper.tex:86`.\n- **Anchor:** “The nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model.”\n- **Rationale:** Seek an authoritative foundation for logistic ability-minus-difficulty responses. Prefer a source also covering 2PL discrimination and item information so it can support the later comparator paragraph. Existing `Andersen1970` supports a distinction about conditional estimation, not this full response-family framing. Broad statistical texts and already-cited LLM compression methods do not directly close the identified gap.\n- **Completion condition:** One verified source supports the Rasch model family. Keep the fixed-ability penalized fitting description explicit; do not portray it as standard joint estimation or cite foundations as evidence of a measured profile benefit.\n\n### 3. Conditional 2PL and Item-Information Complement\n\n- **Query:** `two-parameter logistic item-response model discrimination Fisher item information authoritative reference`\n- **Category:** `baseline_comparator`\n- **Gap / location:** Same `method-rasch-irt-foundations` target; Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability / Comparators and Control Semantics, `paper.tex:130`, and Supplementary Information / Local Reference Implementations, `paper.tex:293`.\n- **Anchor:** “The IRT reference fits a penalized two-parameter logistic response model, chooses items by fitted information near zero ability, and infers target ability from queried responses.”\n- **Rationale:** Seek at most one complementary authoritative source for variable discrimination and Fisher item information `a^2 p(1-p)`, verifying consistency with the draft's `theta*a-d` parameterization. This is foundational support for the already-written local comparator, not a new comparator-family search or certification of an official reproduction.\n- **Execution / completion condition:** Run only if the source from Query 2 does not cover the required 2PL distinction and information expression. Otherwise reuse that source and skip this query. Retain all local-fit, optimizer, and non-reproduction disclaimers.\n\n## Exclusions and Stop Rule\n\n- Do not broaden into a bibliography rebalance, generic benchmark-compression search, citation-graph traversal, related-paper recommendations, or searches for all ten benchmarks. Do not repeat HELM or successful comparator retrieval directions.\n- Retain the original plan's prohibition on identifying or accessing the task-prohibited unnamed paper or its authors, title, findings, mirrors, summaries, reviews, presentations, repositories, or discussions. No such identification is needed for these gaps.\n- Do not equate an upstream collection with staged item universes, filtering, counts, snapshot identity, or public benchmark versions; do not conflate staged MMLU with MMLU-Pro.\n- Do not add latent-class/residual-mixture literatures, new acquisition methods, deployment speedups, new benchmark populations, target-adaptive policies, calibrated stopping rules, or architecture-transfer claims.\n- Do not use foundations to certify optimizer correctness, comparator reproduction, target-calibrated uncertainty, profile-component benefit, selector superiority, or the cause of failed qualification.\n- Stop later retrieval after one suitable collection source and one suitable IRT source, adding a second IRT source only if necessary: approximately two to three unique sources total. An unresolved source should remain an explicit gap rather than trigger a broader search.\n\nThis stage writes a plan only. The manuscript, bibliography, reference provenance, original plan, and draft-gap artifacts remain unchanged.\n@misc{Vivek2024,\n title = {Anchor Points: Benchmarking Models with Much Fewer Examples},\n author = {Rajan Vivek and Kawin Ethayarajh and Diyi Yang and Douwe Kiela},\n year = {2024},\n howpublished = {Conference of the European Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics},\n doi = {10.48550/arXiv.2309.08638}\n}\n\n@misc{Polo2024,\n title = {tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples},\n author = {Felipe Maia Polo and Lucas Weber and Leshem Choshen and Yuekai Sun and Gongjun Xu and Mikhail Yurochkin},\n year = {2024},\n howpublished = {arXiv (Cornell University)},\n doi = {10.48550/arXiv.2402.14992}\n}\n\n@misc{Kipnis2025,\n title = {metabench -- A Sparse Benchmark of Reasoning and Knowledge in Large Language Models},\n author = {Alex Kipnis and Konstantinos Voudouris and Luca M. Schulze Buschoff and Eric Schulz},\n year = {2024},\n howpublished = {arXiv (Cornell University)},\n doi = {10.48550/arXiv.2407.12844}\n}\n\n@misc{Pacchiardi2024,\n title = {100 instances is all you need: predicting the success of a new LLM on unseen data by testing on a few instances},\n author = {Lorenzo Pacchiardi and Lucy G. Cheke and José Hernández‐Orallo},\n year = {2024},\n howpublished = {arXiv (Cornell University)},\n doi = {10.48550/arXiv.2409.03563}\n}\n\n@misc{Li2025,\n title = {Active Evaluation Acquisition for Efficient LLM B