{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"wc -l paper.tex references.bib postdraft_references.bib; cat draft_reference_gaps.md; cat postdraft_reference_query_plan.md; sed -n '1,210p' paper.tex; cat claims_grounding.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"wc -l paper.tex references.bib postdraft_references.bib; cat draft_reference_gaps.md; cat postdraft_reference_query_plan.md; sed -n '1,210p' paper.tex; cat claims_grounding.json\"","aggregated_output":"  422 paper.tex\n  199 references.bib\n  199 postdraft_references.bib\n  820 total\n# Draft Reference Gaps\n\n**Status:** ready\n\nTwo paragraph-specific gaps justify a small retrieval pass for Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark context and direct Rasch/IRT foundations. Existing related-work coverage needs no expansion. Both targets are medium priority; neither requires changing the paper's evidence-bounded thesis.\n\n## Grounded Review\n\nReviewed `paper.tex`, all 25 entries in `references.bib` and their existing provenance, the 30 claims in `claims_grounding.json`, the structure and final-evaluation evidence in `paper_data.json`, `paper_strategy.json`, and `global_memory_summary.md`. All manuscript citation keys resolve, and every existing bibliography entry is cited. Global memory was used only as qualitative context, not numerical evidence. No references were retrieved.\n\n## 1. Benchmark Collection Context\n\n- **Target ID:** `evaluation-leaderboard-context`\n- **Section:** Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability\n- **Paragraph:** Fixed Benchmarks and Grouped Model Holdouts, opening paragraph.\n- **Priority / intent:** medium / benchmark_context\n- **Anchor:** “four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math)”\n- **Gap:** The paragraph identifies a named upstream collection without a direct collection reference; no dedicated Leaderboard source appears in the bibliography. HELM is already represented by `Liang2023`, which can be reused here without new retrieval. `Wang2024` concerns MMLU-Pro and must not be repurposed as provenance for the staged MMLU case.\n- **Focused search topic:** Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark collection documentation covering BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math.\n- **Bounded intervention:** Add one authoritative collection citation. Retain the staged names and the explicit warning against assuming public-benchmark identity. Case counts, fixed item universes, snapshot identity, and filtering remain supported by the supplied artifacts, not by this citation. Do not commission separate searches for all ten benchmarks.\n\n## 2. Named Item-Response Model Foundations\n\n- **Target ID:** `method-rasch-irt-foundations`\n- **Section:** Method\n- **Paragraph:** Linear and Residual-Profile Reconstruction, introduction of Rasch smoothing.\n- **Priority / intent:** medium / background\n- **Anchor:** “The nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model.”\n- **Gap:** The named response family lacks a direct foundational citation. `Andersen1970` is cited in Related Work for an estimation procedure explicitly not used here, and its stored abstract addresses conditional estimation. Broad statistical texts and the already cited LLM compression methods do not directly anchor the Rasch/2PL distinction in these paragraphs. The local IRT comparator in Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability also introduces two-parameter logistic responses and fitted item information without a direct foundation citation.\n- **Focused search topics:** Rasch logistic ability–difficulty model foundations; two-parameter logistic discrimination and Fisher item information.\n- **Bounded intervention:** Retrieve one authoritative source covering both families, or at most two complementary sources. Cite the Rasch introduction and reuse the relevant source at the local IRT comparator if supported. Preserve the fixed-ability, penalized custom-fit description and the disclaimer that local references are not official reproductions. No claim of profile-component benefit or target-calibrated uncertainty should be added.\n\n## Coverage That Does Not Justify Retrieval\n\n- Related Work directly cites Anchor Points, tinyBenchmarks, metabench, reference-instance prediction, and active evaluation acquisition. There is no missing comparator-family paragraph to expand.\n- Scalar-target design, ridge regression, validation dependence, domain shift, missingness, ranking statistics, and bootstrap background already have relevant citations. The selector is explicitly a heuristic, not an unsupported optimality theorem.\n- Numerical accuracy, availability masks, failed qualification, structural diagnostics, and local implementation details are grounded in the supplied evidence. More citations cannot repair missing predictions, isolate a profile contribution, or establish overall superiority.\n- Do not add literature on deployment speedups, new benchmark populations, target-adaptive policies, or calibrated stopping rules: those would expand rather than support the existing story.\n\n## Handoff\n\nRetrieve approximately two to three sources total across these two targets, preferring reuse when one IRT source covers both model families. This memo does not prescribe new paper sections or a broad rewrite. The manuscript, bibliography, and reference provenance remain unchanged.\n# Post-Draft Reference Query Plan\n\n**Status:** ready  \n**Scope:** Three focused query specifications for the two medium-priority gaps in `draft_reference_gaps.md` and `draft_reference_gaps.json`. Query 3 is conditional. A later retrieval pass should add approximately two to three unique sources, not refresh the bibliography. No references were retrieved in this stage.\n\n## Central Topic\n\nEvidence-bounded prediction of LLM full binary benchmark scores and rankings from fixed 8-, 16-, or 32-item prefixes using source-only score-covariance selection and ridge/Rasch-residual reconstruction. Only upstream Open LLM Leaderboard collection context and direct Rasch/2PL foundations need incremental citation support.\n\n## Grounding and Existing Coverage\n\nThe gap memo and JSON were read first and drive every query below. The manuscript, all 25 existing bibliography entries and their stored provenance, the original query-plan schema, and the global memory summary were checked locally. Global memory supplies qualitative context only, not numerical evidence.\n\n- **Core methods:** Fixed source-only prefixes, grouped tuning, and ridge prediction already have adequate methodological context. The Rasch introduction at `paper.tex:86` needs direct model-family support; its fixed-ability, group-weighted penalized fit and optional profile blend remain custom, artifact-grounded details.\n- **Comparators:** The local 2PL comparator at `paper.tex:130` and its information formula at `paper.tex:293` need foundational support. Anchor Points, tinyBenchmarks, metabench, reference-instance prediction, and active evaluation acquisition already have direct citations; do not expand their coverage.\n- **Benchmarks:** One collection reference is missing for the four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases named at `paper.tex:115`. For the six HELM-derived cases in that paragraph, reuse `Liang2023` without retrieval. `Wang2024` addresses MMLU-Pro and is not provenance for staged MMLU.\n- **Architectural shifts:** None are requested or justified.\n- **Result-driven boundaries:** Missing predictions, failed qualification, structural diagnostics, and retained-support results remain artifact-grounded. Citations cannot repair those outcomes, isolate a profile contribution, establish selector superiority, or calibrate target uncertainty.\n\nThe original plan already included a Leaderboard collection query, but no dedicated collection source is present in the resulting bibliography or provenance. Reopening that specific unresolved gap is warranted; rerunning successful comparator searches is not. The stored abstract for `Andersen1970` concerns conditional maximum-likelihood estimation, explicitly distinguished from this implementation, rather than directly supporting both response-model families.\n\n## Queries\n\n### 1. Upstream Leaderboard Collection Context\n\n- **Query:** `Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark collection documentation BBH GPQA MuSR OpenLLM Math`\n- **Category:** `benchmark_context`\n- **Gap / location:** `evaluation-leaderboard-context`; Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability / Fixed Benchmarks and Grouped Model Holdouts, opening paragraph, `paper.tex:115`.\n- **Anchor:** “four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math)”\n- **Rationale:** Seek one authoritative collection source documenting the upstream benchmark composition, closing the unresolved collection citation gap. Do not use it to substantiate staged counts, filtering, exact snapshot identity, or equivalence with public benchmark versions. Reuse existing HELM support instead of searching again; do not substitute MMLU-Pro provenance for staged MMLU.\n- **Completion condition:** One verified collection source supports the named upstream context. Preserve staged case names and the manuscript's explicit non-equivalence warning; do not commission separate searches for every benchmark.\n\n### 2. Rasch Response-Model Foundations\n\n- **Query:** `Rasch logistic item-response model ability item difficulty foundational reference`\n- **Category:** `core_method`\n- **Gap / location:** `method-rasch-irt-foundations`; Method / Linear and Residual-Profile Reconstruction, `paper.tex:86`.\n- **Anchor:** “The nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model.”\n- **Rationale:** Seek an authoritative foundation for logistic ability-minus-difficulty responses. Prefer a source also covering 2PL discrimination and item information so it can support the later comparator paragraph. Existing `Andersen1970` supports a distinction about conditional estimation, not this full response-family framing. Broad statistical texts and already-cited LLM compression methods do not directly close the identified gap.\n- **Completion condition:** One verified source supports the Rasch model family. Keep the fixed-ability penalized fitting description explicit; do not portray it as standard joint estimation or cite foundations as evidence of a measured profile benefit.\n\n### 3. Conditional 2PL and Item-Information Complement\n\n- **Query:** `two-parameter logistic item-response model discrimination Fisher item information authoritative reference`\n- **Category:** `baseline_comparator`\n- **Gap / location:** Same `method-rasch-irt-foundations` target; Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability / Comparators and Control Semantics, `paper.tex:130`, and Supplementary Information / Local Reference Implementations, `paper.tex:293`.\n- **Anchor:** “The IRT reference fits a penalized two-parameter logistic response model, chooses items by fitted information near zero ability, and infers target ability from queried responses.”\n- **Rationale:** Seek at most one complementary authoritative source for variable discrimination and Fisher item information `a^2 p(1-p)`, verifying consistency with the draft's `theta*a-d` parameterization. This is foundational support for the already-written local comparator, not a new comparator-family search or certification of an official reproduction.\n- **Execution / completion condition:** Run only if the source from Query 2 does not cover the required 2PL distinction and information expression. Otherwise reuse that source and skip this query. Retain all local-fit, optimizer, and non-reproduction disclaimers.\n\n## Exclusions and Stop Rule\n\n- Do not broaden into a bibliography rebalance, generic benchmark-compression search, citation-graph traversal, related-paper recommendations, or searches for all ten benchmarks. Do not repeat HELM or successful comparator retrieval directions.\n- Retain the original plan's prohibition on identifying or accessing the task-prohibited unnamed paper or its authors, title, findings, mirrors, summaries, reviews, presentations, repositories, or discussions. No such identification is needed for these gaps.\n- Do not equate an upstream collection with staged item universes, filtering, counts, snapshot identity, or public benchmark versions; do not conflate staged MMLU with MMLU-Pro.\n- Do not add latent-class/residual-mixture literatures, new acquisition methods, deployment speedups, new benchmark populations, target-adaptive policies, calibrated stopping rules, or architecture-transfer claims.\n- Do not use foundations to certify optimizer correctness, comparator reproduction, target-calibrated uncertainty, profile-component benefit, selector superiority, or the cause of failed qualification.\n- Stop later retrieval after one suitable collection source and one suitable IRT source, adding a second IRT source only if necessary: approximately two to three unique sources total. An unresolved source should remain an explicit gap rather than trigger a broader search.\n\nThis stage writes a plan only. The manuscript, bibliography, reference provenance, original plan, and draft-gap artifacts remain unchanged.\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Score-Targeted Item Selection for LLM Benchmark Prediction:\\\\Accuracy and Evaluation Limits}\n\\author{}\n\\newcommand{\\clip}{\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,1]}}\n\\newcommand{\\sigmoid}{\\operatorname{sigmoid}}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nPredicting a language model's benchmark score from a few responses can reduce evaluation cost, but prediction accuracy and evaluation completeness are different requirements. We study a source-only estimator that selects items by their conditional covariance with the full score and combines ridge prediction with an optional Rasch-residual profile mixture. A frozen evaluation covers ten fixed benchmark cases, three grouped model splits, and budgets of 8, 16, and 32 items. On common available support, the estimator's mean absolute error decreases from 0.0439 to 0.0278 across these budgets; its Spearman correlation increases from 0.7787 to 0.8858. These are conditional descriptions, not qualified overall results. The evaluation completed but failed qualification: primary and reference predictions are missing for two Commonsense splits, and matched structural checks fail. The reported aggregate MAE of 0.0880 includes missing-output penalties. We separate measured prediction errors, scoring defaults, and unresolved diagnostics, establishing an evidence-bounded assessment rather than an overall selection-advantage claim.\n\\end{abstract}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation]]\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/budget_mae]]\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/budget_spearman]]\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nFull-benchmark evaluation repeatedly measures the same item universe across language models, checkpoints, or inference settings. Historical item-level responses may therefore contain useful information about which questions distinguish model behavior. The practical question is whether a new model can answer a small, fixed subset while a source-trained estimator reconstructs its full score and preserves model rankings. This setting differs from accelerating generation: the savings come from querying fewer items, not from changing the evaluated language model.\n\nExisting benchmark-compression methods exploit response similarity, latent ability, or predictive dependencies \\citep{Vivek2024,Polo2024,Kipnis2025,Pacchiardi2024,Li2025}. A complementary design objective is to select questions directly for the scalar score of interest. An item can distinguish source response patterns without being especially informative about their average. Conversely, correlated items can remain useful if their residual variation predicts that average. This motivates conditioning on already selected items and greedily targeting the remaining covariance with the full source score.\n\nWe study this design together with a source-trained reconstruction family. A linear ridge predictor provides a simple fallback; a group-balanced mixture of Rasch-shrunk source profiles offers nonlinear response-pattern matching. Both use the same nested item prefixes, and their combination is selected using source-group holdouts. The scientific hypothesis is that source response structure can improve score reconstruction at a fixed query budget. The available evaluation does not isolate a benefit from the profile component, nor does it establish a generally advantageous selector.\n\nThe decisive evidence boundary is evaluation integrity. The frozen assessment completed but did not qualify. Missing primary predictions coincide with missing reference predictions in part of the Commonsense evidence; matched structural checks also fail there. The retained predictions can describe accuracy on available pairs, but cannot supply the missing outcomes or restore qualification. Treating penalty-scored aggregate errors as ordinary prediction errors would obscure this distinction.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nOur contributions are consequently bounded: a precise description of a score-targeted, source-only reconstruction method; a common-support comparison across fixed benchmarks and item budgets; and an explicit account of how incomplete records restrict the interpretation of those comparisons. We report the unsuccessful qualification alongside the conditional results rather than selecting whichever summary gives the most favorable ordering. The resulting study addresses what these measurements establish, and what remains unmeasured.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/method_and_evidence_overview.png}\n\\caption{Source-only item design and score reconstruction, with an explicit evidence boundary. Source responses determine a fixed nested item order, ridge fitting, and an optional residual-profile mixture. Target responses update prediction only. Measured prediction error is distinguished from missing records and failed checks; the latter cannot be repaired by conditioning on available predictions.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Few-item language-model evaluation.}\nAnchor Points selects a small set of representative examples from cross-model response structure \\citep{Vivek2024}. tinyBenchmarks studies efficient score estimation with curated subsets, and metabench uses latent abilities to compress reasoning and knowledge evaluation \\citep{Polo2024,Kipnis2025}. Reference-instance assessors predict model performance on other instances \\citep{Pacchiardi2024}; learned evaluation-acquisition policies model dependencies between test outcomes \\citep{Li2025}. Our fixed source-only order is not target-adaptive acquisition. The local anchor and IRT references below are explicitly defined implementations, not certified reproductions of these published systems.\n\n\\paragraph{Design and reconstruction.}\nChoosing observations for a scalar target relates to optimal allocation in regression \\citep{Elfving1952}, whereas information criteria for a whole parameter vector address a different objective \\citep{Kiefer1960}. Our empirical covariance rule is a regularized linear-prediction heuristic, not an optimal-design theorem for shifted binary responses. Ridge regression supplies a stabilized linear estimator \\citep{Hoerl1970}; standard statistical-learning and probabilistic-modeling texts provide broader background \\citep{Hastie2009,Bishop2006}. The profile model is a finite mixture, but it does not fit mixture weights by expectation maximization \\citep{Dempster1977}. Likewise, its fixed-ability difficulty fitting should not be confused with conditional maximum-likelihood estimation \\citep{Andersen1970}.\n\n\\paragraph{Assessment under dependence and missingness.}\nCross-validation distinguishes choosing a predictor from assessing it \\citep{Stone1974,Arlot2010}; evaluating a configuration on the same validation evidence used to tune it can be optimistic \\citep{Varma2006}. Grouped validation addresses dependence, but the blocking scheme determines which extrapolation question is tested \\citep{Roberts2017}. Domain-shift theory further motivates caution about transferring source accuracy to new response distributions \\citep{BenDavid2010}. Missing-data inference requires assumptions about the missingness process \\citep{Rubin1976,Little2019}; we make no such assumptions here. HELM motivates multi-metric evaluation \\citep{Liang2023}, and distinct benchmark constructions, such as MMLU-Pro, should not be conflated with the fixed MMLU case studied here \\citep{Wang2024}. These distinctions motivate reporting error, ranking, and evidence availability separately.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem Setup and Design Rationale}\nLet $X\\in\\{0,1\\}^{S\\times N}$ contain complete item responses of source models, and let $y\\in\\{0,1\\}^{N}$ denote a target model's responses. Its reference score is $s(y)=N^{-1}\\sum_{j=1}^{N}y_j$. Only $y_Q$ is observable to the estimator, for a subset $Q$ of size $b\\ll N$. Source rows have supplied group labels $g(i)$; target identities, target groups, and full target scores are unavailable during fitting and prediction. The goal is a bounded estimate $\\hat s_b(y_Q)$ with small absolute and squared score error and accurate target-model ordering.\n\nThe studied budgets are $b\\in\\{8,16,32\\}$. A single source-only order gives nested prefixes $Q_b$, with no repeated items. This is not a claim that these budgets suffice for every benchmark. The rationale is to combine task-directed acquisition with conservative reconstruction: selection targets the full-score functional, while a tuned linear fallback limits reliance on an overly specific source-profile model. Equal total source-group mass reduces the influence of densely represented groups in selection and profile priors, without asserting that groups perfectly recover training ancestry.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/budgets]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L1-L28]]\n\n\\subsection{Score-Targeted Fixed Prefixes}\nFor $G$ source groups with sizes $n_g$, define $w_i=(G n_{g(i)})^{-1}$, source item means $\\mu_j=\\sum_i w_iX_{ij}$, and full source scores $t_i=N^{-1}\\sum_jX_{ij}$. With $\\bar t=\\sum_iw_it_i$, form\n\\begin{equation}\n Z_{ij}=\\sqrt{w_i}(X_{ij}-\\mu_j),\\qquad\n u_i=\\sqrt{w_i}(t_i-\\bar t),\\quad\n C=Z^\\top Z+D,\\quad c=Z^\\top u,\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $D_{jj}=0.1\\max\\{\\mu_j(1-\\mu_j),0.05\\}$. For the selected prefix $Q$, residual covariance and variance are\n\\begin{equation}\n c_{j\\mid Q}=c_j-C_{jQ}C_{QQ}^{-1}c_Q,\\qquad\n v_{j\\mid Q}=C_{jj}-C_{jQ}C_{QQ}^{-1}C_{Qj}.\n\\end{equation}\nThe next item maximizes $c_{j\\mid Q}^{\\,2}/\\max(v_{j\\mid Q},10^{-12})$ among unselected items. This is the reduction in residual variance for a linear scalar-score predictor under the empirical covariance model. It is neither an information guarantee for the true target distribution nor a causal statement about particular questions.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L57-L91]]\n\nThe implementation updates residual quantities through a low-rank pivoted factorization instead of materializing the dense item covariance. Selection is rebuilt using only training groups inside every source-validation fold. The final fit repeats it on all supplied source rows. Crucially, observing target answers never changes the item order or its hyperparameters.\n\n\\subsection{Linear and Residual-Profile Reconstruction}\nThe linear branch regresses source full scores on the selected binary responses. Centering features at their unweighted source mean $\\bar x_Q$ and scores at $\\bar t_{\\rm row}$ gives\n\\begin{equation}\n \\beta_b=(X_{Q,c}^{\\top}X_{Q,c}+\\lambda I)^{-1}X_{Q,c}^{\\top}(t-\\bar t_{\\rm row}),\\qquad\n \\hat s^{\\rm lin}_b=\\clip\\!\\left[\\bar t_{\\rm row}+\\gamma(y_Q-\\bar x_Q)^\\top\\beta_b\\right].\n\\end{equation}\nThe ridge fit itself is row-unweighted; group balancing is used for selecting configurations, not silently substituted into this regression. Per-budget $\\lambda\\in\\{0.1,1,10\\}$ and $\\gamma\\in\\{0,0.5,1\\}$ minimize source-held-out group-weighted squared error.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L94-L100]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L200-L270]]\n\nThe nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model. Source abilities are fixed at $\\theta_i=\\operatorname{logit}[(\\sum_jX_{ij}+0.5)/(N+1)]$. Item difficulties $d_j$ are fitted by group-weighted Bernoulli likelihood with a quadratic penalty. Write $r_{ij}=\\sigmoid(\\theta_i-d_j)$ and\n\\begin{equation}\n p_{ij}=\\operatorname{clip}_{[\\epsilon,1-\\epsilon]}\n       [(1-\\rho)r_{ij}+\\rho X_{ij}],\\qquad\n p_{i\\ell j}=\\sigmoid[\\operatorname{logit}(p_{ij})+a_\\ell].\n\\end{equation}\nThe offsets are $a_\\ell=h(-2,-1,0,1,2)_\\ell$ with prior proportions $(1,4,6,4,1)/16$. Each source group's total prior mass is equal; within a group it is divided equally across source rows. Thus this is a mixture of source-derived response profiles and ability offsets, not a new language-model architecture.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L103-L141]]\n\nFor observed responses, a particle $k=(i,\\ell)$ receives posterior mass\n\\begin{equation}\n \\omega_k(y_Q)\\ \\propto\\ \\pi_k\\prod_{j\\in Q}p_{kj}^{y_j}(1-p_{kj})^{1-y_j},\\qquad\n \\hat s^{\\rm prof}_b=\\frac{\\sum_{j\\in Q}y_j+\\sum_k\\omega_k\\sum_{j\\notin Q}p_{kj}}{N}.\n\\label{eq:profile}\n\\end{equation}\nThe observed contribution is exact; only unqueried responses are reconstructed. Log-sum-exp normalization stabilizes the calculation. Every request uses the original prior and the complete current prefix, so cumulative responses are not counted twice. This model-based posterior is not an empirically calibrated uncertainty interval.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L158-L191]]\n\nThe output is $(1-\\alpha)\\hat s^{\\rm lin}_b+\\alpha\\hat s^{\\rm prof}_b$. Source tuning considers $\\rho\\in\\{0,0.5,0.75\\}$, $h\\in\\{0,0.5\\}$, and $\\alpha\\in\\{0,0.5,1\\}$, using at most three group-disjoint folds. It selects one profile/blend configuration across budgets by equal-group held-out MAE. Ties favor smaller blend weight, offset scale, then residual weight. A zero blend is an explicit linear fallback. The final evidence does not record which blend was used in each fit, so it cannot demonstrate a nonzero profile contribution.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L194-L287]]\n\n\\subsection{What a Fixed Prefix Can Identify}\nThe reconstruction problem has an information constraint independent of the chosen statistical model. If two target models have identical queried responses, the interface supplies no information that could distinguish them, so the estimator returns the same value. In particular, neither target identities nor target group memberships are additional inputs to prediction. The source-trained mappings and particle priors are also identical for these rows. A fixed prefix therefore cannot distinguish targets that agree on observed items but differ elsewhere. We do not measure the prevalence of such pairs in the final evidence: the available score records do not supply the queried response patterns needed for that analysis. This source-inspectable property explains why score covariance is a design rationale rather than a target-level guarantee. Undocumented target response patterns may remain indistinguishable even when source-held-out prediction looks accurate.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L336-L365]]\n\nThe two reconstruction branches make different assumptions about this unresolved information. The linear branch extends source score relationships through a regularized affine map. The profile branch assigns probability to source-shaped patterns with ability offsets, then averages their unobserved totals. The convex fallback prevents source tuning from requiring the profile branch, but does not protect against shifts absent from every validation group. Neither branch acquires new target information beyond the prescribed prefix. Increasing the number of particles or retuning the blend therefore cannot by itself resolve identical-prefix ambiguity. Conversely, replacing the item order may change which targets become indistinguishable, but its effect cannot be assigned to acquisition alone when the reconstruction is also refitted. This motivates keeping the procedure-level and matched-order comparisons separate in the results.\n\n\\section{Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability}\n\\subsection{Fixed Benchmarks and Grouped Model Holdouts}\nThe final assessment contains four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math) and six HELM-derived cases (Commonsense, GSM, LegalBench, MATH, MedQA, and MMLU). We retain these staged case names rather than asserting identity with every similarly named public benchmark. Each case uses a fixed binary item universe. The question is prediction for held-out models on that universe, not generalization to newly sampled questions or unseen benchmark distributions.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/final_data_manifest.json]]\n\nSeeds 5003, 7001, and 9001 define model-group splits. The evaluator orders groups by a seed-dependent hash, assigns approximately 70\\% of groups to the source side, and withholds the rest as targets. Group construction uses available provenance and conservative family/provider information. It is a metadata heuristic, not a proof of disjoint pretraining or complete ancestry separation. The estimator sees only opaque source-group labels. Source tuning is internal to a fit; final targets are not used to select its settings. Repeated seeds reuse model populations and are not independent populations.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L685-L708]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/final_data_manifest.json#/split_rule]]\n\n\\subsection{Comparators and Control Semantics}\nThe \\emph{fitted uniform-selection control} replaces the score-targeted order with a seeded uniform nested order, rebuilding selection and tuning in each fold. It keeps the reconstruction family, not necessarily the fitted predictor: hyperparameters can change. The \\emph{selected-item mean} retains the primary order and returns the raw observed mean, removing reconstruction as a whole rather than an affine calibration layer. The \\emph{uniform sample mean} averages responses on the same seeded uniform order used by the fitted uniform control, permitting a second matched-order reconstruction comparison.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L57-L62]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L327-L365]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L774-L840]]\n\nThe anchor reference clusters centered, normalized item response vectors and estimates scores from representatives weighted by assigned cluster sizes. The IRT reference fits a penalized two-parameter logistic response model, chooses items by fitted information near zero ability, and infers target ability from queried responses. These references use the same query budgets, but they are local comparators, not official reproductions of Anchor Points or tinyBenchmarks. A source-mean reference predicts the average source score without target responses. Extended fitting details are in the supplement.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L711-L771]]\n\n\\subsection{Three Distinct Reporting Objects}\nWe distinguish (i) the evaluator's reported aggregate, (ii) prediction errors computed on common available records, and (iii) qualification and structural diagnostics. In the reported aggregate, the two data sources receive equal mass, cases are equally weighted within source, and seeds and budgets are equally weighted. Missing predictions receive worst-case defaults: MAE and RMSE are set to one, and rank correlations to minus one. Absent baseline records similarly receive MAE one. These defaults are operational penalties, not measured model predictions.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L870-L873]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L1005-L1042]]\n\nFor conditional comparisons, all displayed methods must have predictions for a case--seed pair. We average budgets and targets within that pair and show its seed-level values. Pooled conditional summaries retain equal data-source and case weights, but normalize seed weights over available seeds \\emph{within each case}. The same availability intersection is used for error and ranking in the budget view. Commonsense therefore contributes only its available split, rather than being silently omitted or assigned a synthetic loss. This changes the estimand; it does not estimate missing performance. We show descriptive seed dispersion, not confidence bars. Spearman and Kendall correlations describe ordering \\citep{Spearman1904,Kendall1938}; neither establishes score calibration.\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/common_support]]\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Completed Evaluation, Failed Qualification}\nThe final evaluation completed and failed qualification. Table~\\ref{tab:aggregate} preserves its scored summaries. Primary MAE is 0.0880 and RMSE is 0.0979; reported Spearman and Kendall correlations are 0.7329 and 0.5943. These summaries include defaults for missing primary records. Their ordering against the fitted uniform-selection MAE of 0.0449 cannot be interpreted as a measured overall selection disadvantage.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Reported final aggregates, \\emph{including evaluator defaults}. Errors are score fractions. Missing primary and baseline records receive error one; fitted controls have different availability. These columns are not a measured-only superiority ranking.}\n\\label{tab:aggregate}\n\\begin{tabular}{lr@{\\qquad}lr}\n\\toprule\nPrediction rule & MAE & Primary diagnostic & Value\\\\\n\\midrule\nScore-targeted reconstruction & 0.0880 & RMSE & 0.0979\\\\\nUniform selection + fitting & 0.0449 & Spearman & 0.7329\\\\\nSelected-item mean & 0.1084 & Kendall $\\tau_b$ & 0.5943\\\\\nUniform sample mean & 0.1336 & Structural-control failure summary & 0.0417\\\\\nIRT reference & 0.1187 & Missing-evaluation fraction & 0.0110\\\\\nAnchor reference & 0.2205 & Checked replay discrepancy & 0.0000\\\\\nSource-mean reference & 0.1644 & Qualification & Failed\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n\nThe failure evidence is localized but consequential (Table~\\ref{tab:audit}). Primary predictions and all reference predictions are absent for Commonsense at seeds 5003 and 9001. Constant, copied, permuted, and selected-item-mean conditions have recorded predictions there but fail structural checks. The fitted uniform control is available there. The retained record does not identify the underlying exception or root defect, so missing outputs should not be described as an accuracy failure, and the failed checks should not be attributed to a particular implementation error without additional evidence.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Availability and integrity audit of the final evidence. The two affected Commonsense splits are not counted as measured prediction losses in our conditional figures.}\n\\label{tab:audit}\n\\begin{tabular}{p{.30\\linewidth}p{.60\\linewidth}}\n\\toprule\nEvidence & Recorded finding\\\\\n\\midrule\nCommonsense, seeds 5003/9001 & Primary and reference predictions absent; matched constant, copy, permutation, and selected-mean structural checks fail.\\\\\nCommon comparison support & 28 of 30 case--seed pairs; Commonsense uses seed 7001 only.\\\\\nPaired primary--anchor contrast & $-0.0769$ with stored endpoints $[-1,1]$; endpoints are fallback values, not an informative interval.\\\\\nReset replay & Maximum absolute discrepancy $0$ for the checked replay; not a certificate for other conditions.\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/common_support]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/delta_anchor_mae]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L1040-L1074]]\n\nThe paired primary--anchor contrast has a separate missing-record convention. It is not obtained by subtracting the separately penalized aggregate MAEs. Its stored interval endpoints remain the evaluator's initialized fallback because complete pairing is unavailable. We therefore report no significance claim. Bootstrap methodology \\citep{Efron1979} does not turn unexecuted or unsupported resampling into an uncertainty estimate.\n\n\\subsection{Conditional Comparison Across Fixed Cases}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:cases} compares only common available predictions. The primary estimator has lower descriptive MAE than the fitted uniform control in nine cases; MedQA is the exception (0.0574 versus 0.0530). This count includes Commonsense with only one supported seed. BBH gives 0.0308 versus 0.0570, while the corresponding values on MATH are 0.0603 and 0.0624. Thus the observed differences vary substantially across cases. The anchor reference's especially large MuSR error also illustrates why a single pooled comparator should not substitute for the case-level view.\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/case_mae]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/available_pair_method_comparison.pdf}\n\\caption{Available-pair MAE, averaged equally over query budgets, separated by data source. Small dots show supported seeds; vertical marks show their descriptive mean. Parentheses give supported seed counts; the asterisk marks missing Commonsense records at seeds 5003 and 9001. All four methods use identical case--seed support. Missing-output penalties are not plotted as measured errors. These conditional results do not restore failed qualification.}\n\\label{fig:cases}\n\\end{figure}\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/case_seed_mae]]\n\nThe conditional comparison differs from the reported aggregate ordering because it changes both the available support and the handling of missing records. Neither view should replace the other: the reported aggregate reflects the evaluator's failure policy, while the conditional view describes retained predictions. The latter is compatible with useful prediction accuracy on observed cases and simultaneously insufficient evidence for a qualified overall result. It does not establish that missingness is benign or that the omitted outcomes would follow the same pattern.\n\n\\subsection{Item Budget, Reconstruction, and Ranking}\nOn the stated common-support aggregation, primary MAE is 0.0439, 0.0333, and 0.0278 at the three budgets; fitted uniform-selection MAE is 0.0598, 0.0439, and 0.0328. Figure~\\ref{fig:budgets} separates absolute-score prediction from model ranking. Selected-item raw means have much larger MAE (0.1321, 0.1050, and 0.0851), but Spearman correlations of 0.7806, 0.8461, and 0.8819 are close to the primary values of 0.7787, 0.8485, and 0.8858. Reconstruction therefore changes absolute-score error much more than rank correlation in this retained evidence. This is a descriptive matched-order contrast, not an attribution to any one reconstruction component.\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/budget_mae]]\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/budget_spearman]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The final evaluation completed but did not qualify; no conditional reaggregation changes this status.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.qualification.status\", \"final.qualification.outcome\", \"final.qualification.correct\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The final evidence covers ten fixed benchmark cases, four Leaderboard-derived and six HELM-derived, with seeds 5003, 7001 and 9001 and budgets 8, 16 and 32.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\", \"@evidence[[source_evidence/final_data_manifest.json]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Primary and all reference predictions are absent for Commonsense at seeds 5003 and 9001. Constant, copy, permuted and selected-item-mean structural checks fail there, while fitted uniform selection is available. The underlying exception is not identified by these records.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[derived_results.json#/audit]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Common-support comparisons use 28 of 30 case-seed pairs, with Commonsense supported only at seed 7001. All other cases have three supported seeds.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[derived_results.json#/common_support]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Reported primary aggregate MAE is 0.08801465854359276 and RMSE 0.09789336720441476, including missing-output defaults.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.mae\", \"final.metric.rmse\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L870-L873]]\", \"@evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L1005-L1042]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Reported primary Spearman is 0.7328732236748521 and Kendall tau-b 0.5943160224743321; these include missing-record defaults.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.spearman\", \"final.metric.kendall_tau_b\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Spearman1904\", \"Kendall1938\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Reported fitted-uniform MAE 0.044910877034389785, selected-mean MAE 0.10836174574059959, uniform-mean MAE 0.1335974367939037, IRT MAE 0.1186668212839868, anchor MAE 0.22047382735923254, and source-mean MAE 0.1643825693696333 have different missing-output exposure and do not form a measured-only superiority ranking.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.no_diversity_mae\", \"final.metric.no_calibration_mae\", \"final.metric.uniform_mae\", \"final.metric.irt_mae\", \"final.metric.anchor_mae\", \"final.metric.source_mean_mae\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Structural-control failure summary is 0.041666666666666664, missing-evaluation fraction is 0.011049723756906077, and checked replay maximum discrepancy is zero. The first two are evaluator-defined summaries, not rates over independent target models or case-seed pairs.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.control_failure_rate\", \"final.metric.missing_episode_rate\", \"final.metric.reset_max_abs_error\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L1070-L1076]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The stored primary-minus-anchor contrast is -0.0769036132600842 with endpoints [-1,1]; these are fallback endpoints because pairing is incomplete, not an informative confidence interval. Its missing-record convention differs from separately penalized marginal MAEs.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.delta_anchor_mae\", \"final.metric.delta_anchor_ci_low\", \"final.metric.delta_anchor_ci_high\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Efron1979\"],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L1034-L1068]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Available-pair primary MAE is lower than fitted-uniform MAE in nine cases; MedQA is the exception at 0.0574 versus 0.0530. This is descriptive and includes singleton-support Commonsense.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[derived_results.json#/case_mae]]\", \"@evidence[[build_paper_artifacts.py]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Conditional BBH primary and fitted-uniform MAEs are 0.0308 and 0.0570, and MATH MAEs are 0.0603 and 0.0624. MuSR anchor error is heterogeneous relative to the other cases.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[derived_results.json#/case_mae]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Under equal-source/equal-case and within-case available-seed normalization, primary MAEs at budgets 8/16/32 are 0.0438507255604756, 0.03333117402076665 and 0.027776433747489636.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[derived_results.json#/budget_mae/0]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"On identical common support, fitted-uniform MAEs at budgets 8/16/32 are 0.05983060181043318, 0.04388863282142454 and 0.03283671741622329.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[derived_results.json#/budget_mae/1]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Selected-item mean MAEs at budgets 8/16/32 are 0.13207754853140033, 0.10495817584576625 and 0.08507833899220836; uniform-mean MAEs are 0.10821494499086409, 0.08229789663800609 and 0.05556974653061864. Uniform raw means have lower MAE at every displayed budget, whereas fitted selection reverses the conditional ordering.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[derived_results.json#/budget_mae]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Conditional primary Spearman correlations at budgets 8/16/32 are 0.7787133623969075, 0.8485275353348883 and 0.8858252382784845. Selected-mean correlations are 0.7805746798716318, 0.8461155247695583 and 0.8818752839767907. Reconstruction changes absolute-score error much more than ranking in this retained evidence, without isolating a particular reconstruction component.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[derived_results.json#/budget_spearman]]\", \"@evidence[[derived_results.json#/budget_mae]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"All numerical cells of supplementary case and budget tables and both quantitative figures are deterministic transformations of recorded final target means and predictions on their declared common support. Seed dots are descriptive, with overlapping model populations, and are not confidence intervals.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[derived_results.json]]\", \"@evidence[[build_paper_artifacts.py]]\", \"@evidence[[tables/case_mae.tex]]\", \"@evidence[[tables/budget_metrics.tex]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The supplementary seed table retains every primary case-seed record, recorded target/group counts, and budget-averaged MAE/RMSE/Spearman/Kendall. Missing rows remain missing. The exhaustive CSV separates each budget, condition and reference with availability/structural status, using the evaluator's zero rank-correlation convention for exactly constant vectors.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[tables/primary_seed_metrics.tex]]\", \"@evidence[[complete_metrics.csv]]\", \"@evidence[[build_paper_artifacts.py]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The remaining reported diagnostic MAEs are 0.22204546910278564 for constant observations, 0.15448451324975213 for copied observations, 0.1005045540030643 for permuted observations and 0.02807015578570595 for the single checked replay; they are not ordinary independent target-accuracy estimates.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.constant_mae\", \"final.metric.copy_mae\", \"final.metric.permuted_mae\", \"final.metric.reset_mae\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The evaluated source-only selector uses equal total group weights, empirical score covariance, diagonal nugget 0.1 max(mu(1-mu),0.05), and greedy squared conditional covariance divided by conditional item variance floored at 1e-12. It uses low-rank updates and no target-adaptive acquisition.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Elfving1952\", \"Kiefer1960\"],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L52-L91]]\"],\n      \"grounding_note\": \"Registry id identifies the delivered configuration only; readable code is substantive support, not the metric value or hash.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The linear branch fits row-unweighted centered ridge regression with an intercept and shrinkage. Per-budget strength grid is 0.1/1/10 and shrinkage grid 0/0.5/1, selected by group-weighted source-held-out squared error.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Hoerl1970\"],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L94-L100]]\", \"@evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L201-L270]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The source profile branch fixes smoothed-logit abilities, initializes difficulties from clipped group-weighted item means, and uses eight bounded Newton updates with difficulty penalty 0.01. It is not joint or conditional maximum-likelihood Rasch estimation.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Andersen1970\"],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L103-L115]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Profiles interpolate Rasch probabilities with binary source responses and clip at 1e-6. Each source contributes five ability-offset particles with offsets h*(-2,-1,0,1,2) and prior proportions (1,4,6,4,1)/16; total mass is equal per source group.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L118-L155]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Posterior normalization uses log-sum-exp and adds the exact observed total to the expected unqueried total. Every prefix recomputes from the original prior; the posterior is not a calibrated empirical uncertainty interval.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L158-L191]]\", \"@evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L336-L365]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"At most three group-disjoint source folds choose residual weight 0/0.5/0.75, offset scale 0/0.5 and blend 0/0.5/1, by group-balanced MAE across budgets, with simpler tie-breaking. One-group fallback is ridge strength one, shrinkage one and zero profile blend. No final fitted blend logs or independent final component ablations are available.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Varma2006\", \"Arlot2010\", \"Stone1974\"],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L201-L287]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The uniform fitted control changes acquisition and refits/tunes reconstruction. The selected-mean control returns the raw mean on the primary order. The uniform mean uses the same prescribed seeded uniform order as the fitted uniform control. These compare configured procedures or whole reconstruction, not isolated particle or selector effects.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"result.top_program_01\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L57-L62]]\", \"@evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L327-L365]]\", \"@evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L774-L793]]\", \"@evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L924-L944]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The evaluator uses seed-hashed approximately 70/30 model-group splits and equal source-domain, within-domain case, seed and budget weights. Metadata grouping is conservative and cannot prove full training-lineage separation.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Roberts2017\", \"BenDavid2010\"],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L685-L708]]\", \"@evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L1005-L1018]]\", \"@evidence[[source_evidence/final_data_manifest.json]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The anchor reference clusters centered normalized response vectors using one initialization and at most 30 iterations; scores are representative responses weighted by nearest-representative assignments. This is a local reference, not an exact published reproduction.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Vivek2024\"],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L711-L729]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The IRT reference uses a penalized 2PL model, L-BFGS-B with at most 40 iterations and information-ranked fixed items, followed by up to 30 regularized Newton ability updates. It averages full-universe model probabilities and is not an exact tinyBenchmarks reproduction.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Polo2024\"],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L732-L771]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Identical queried response rows receive the same estimator output without target identity/group inputs. The prevalence of indistinguishable target pairs is not measured; this is a source-inspectable interface property, not a new theorem or empirical finding.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L336-L365]]\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Prior permitted work covers representative anchors, efficient benchmark score estimation, sparse ability-based benchmarks, reference-instance assessors and learned evaluation acquisition. These citations provide context, not performance measurements for this study.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W4411630215\", \"ref.openalex.W4392181972\", \"ref.openalex.W4406058560\", \"ref.openalex.W4403556531\", \"ref.openalex.W4403344243\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Vivek2024\", \"Polo2024\", \"Kipnis2025\", \"Pacchiardi2024\", \"Li2025\"],\n      \"grounding_note\": \"Permitted exact-DOI metadata and abstracts were inspected; see reference_consultation.md. No citation-graph traversal or prohibited-source identification was used.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^(@|'\"\\\\\\\\section|\\\\\\\\subsection)|Rasch model|four Open LLM|two-parameter|citation|qualification' paper.tex references.bib postdraft_references.bib | head -110; ls .codex/skills/{validate-paper,write-paper-standalone,scientific-evidence,memory-log}/scripts; ls -la global_memory; cat local_validation_report.json; head -65 .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat postdraft_reference_retrieval.log | tail -25\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^(@|'\"\\\\\\\\section|\\\\\\\\subsection)|Rasch model|four Open LLM|two-parameter|citation|qualification' paper.tex references.bib postdraft_references.bib | head -110; ls .codex/skills/{validate-paper,write-paper-standalone,scientific-evidence,memory-log}/scripts; ls -la global_memory; cat local_validation_report.json; head -65 .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat postdraft_reference_retrieval.log | tail -25\"","aggregated_output":"postdraft_references.bib:1:@misc{Vivek2024,\npostdraft_references.bib:9:@misc{Polo2024,\npostdraft_references.bib:17:@misc{Kipnis2025,\npostdraft_references.bib:25:@misc{Pacchiardi2024,\npostdraft_references.bib:33:@misc{Li2025,\npostdraft_references.bib:41:@misc{Liang2023,\npostdraft_references.bib:49:@misc{Wang2024,\npostdraft_references.bib:57:@misc{Hoerl1970,\npostdraft_references.bib:65:@misc{Elfving1952,\npostdraft_references.bib:73:@misc{Kiefer1960,\npostdraft_references.bib:81:@misc{Andersen1970,\npostdraft_references.bib:89:@misc{Dempster1977,\npostdraft_references.bib:97:@misc{Hastie2009,\npostdraft_references.bib:105:@misc{Bishop2006,\npostdraft_references.bib:113:@misc{Efron1979,\npostdraft_references.bib:121:@misc{Rubin1976,\npostdraft_references.bib:129:@misc{Little2019,\npostdraft_references.bib:137:@misc{Roberts2017,\npostdraft_references.bib:145:@misc{Varma2006,\npostdraft_references.bib:153:@misc{Arlot2010,\npostdraft_references.bib:161:@misc{Stone1974,\npostdraft_references.bib:169:@misc{Kendall1938,\npostdraft_references.bib:177:@misc{Spearman1904,\npostdraft_references.bib:185:@misc{BenDavid2010,\npostdraft_references.bib:193:@misc{Brier1950,\npaper.tex:15:Predicting a language model's benchmark score from a few responses can reduce evaluation cost, but prediction accuracy and evaluation completeness are different requirements. We study a source-only estimator that selects items by their conditional covariance with the full score and combines ridge prediction with an optional Rasch-residual profile mixture. A frozen evaluation covers ten fixed benchmark cases, three grouped model splits, and budgets of 8, 16, and 32 items. On common available support, the estimator's mean absolute error decreases from 0.0439 to 0.0278 across these budgets; its Spearman correlation increases from 0.7787 to 0.8858. These are conditional descriptions, not qualified overall results. The evaluation completed but failed qualification: primary and reference predictions are missing for two Commonsense splits, and matched structural checks fail. The reported aggregate MAE of 0.0880 includes missing-output penalties. We separate measured prediction errors, scoring defaults, and unresolved diagnostics, establishing an evidence-bounded assessment rather than an overall selection-advantage claim.\npaper.tex:21:\\section{Introduction}\npaper.tex:28:The decisive evidence boundary is evaluation integrity. The frozen assessment completed but did not qualify. Missing primary predictions coincide with missing reference predictions in part of the Commonsense evidence; matched structural checks also fail there. The retained predictions can describe accuracy on available pairs, but cannot supply the missing outcomes or restore qualification. Treating penalty-scored aggregate errors as ordinary prediction errors would obscure this distinction.\npaper.tex:32:Our contributions are consequently bounded: a precise description of a score-targeted, source-only reconstruction method; a common-support comparison across fixed benchmarks and item budgets; and an explicit account of how incomplete records restrict the interpretation of those comparisons. We report the unsuccessful qualification alongside the conditional results rather than selecting whichever summary gives the most favorable ordering. The resulting study addresses what these measurements establish, and what remains unmeasured.\npaper.tex:41:\\section{Related Work}\npaper.tex:51:\\section{Method}\npaper.tex:52:\\subsection{Problem Setup and Design Rationale}\npaper.tex:59:\\subsection{Score-Targeted Fixed Prefixes}\npaper.tex:76:\\subsection{Linear and Residual-Profile Reconstruction}\npaper.tex:86:The nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model. Source abilities are fixed at $\\theta_i=\\operatorname{logit}[(\\sum_jX_{ij}+0.5)/(N+1)]$. Item difficulties $d_j$ are fitted by group-weighted Bernoulli likelihood with a quadratic penalty. Write $r_{ij}=\\sigmoid(\\theta_i-d_j)$ and\npaper.tex:107:\\subsection{What a Fixed Prefix Can Identify}\npaper.tex:113:\\section{Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability}\npaper.tex:114:\\subsection{Fixed Benchmarks and Grouped Model Holdouts}\npaper.tex:115:The final assessment contains four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math) and six HELM-derived cases (Commonsense, GSM, LegalBench, MATH, MedQA, and MMLU). We retain these staged case names rather than asserting identity with every similarly named public benchmark. Each case uses a fixed binary item universe. The question is prediction for held-out models on that universe, not generalization to newly sampled questions or unseen benchmark distributions.\npaper.tex:124:\\subsection{Comparators and Control Semantics}\npaper.tex:130:The anchor reference clusters centered, normalized item response vectors and estimates scores from representatives weighted by assigned cluster sizes. The IRT reference fits a penalized two-parameter logistic response model, chooses items by fitted information near zero ability, and infers target ability from queried responses. These references use the same query budgets, but they are local comparators, not official reproductions of Anchor Points or tinyBenchmarks. A source-mean reference predicts the average source score without target responses. Extended fitting details are in the supplement.\npaper.tex:133:\\subsection{Three Distinct Reporting Objects}\npaper.tex:134:We distinguish (i) the evaluator's reported aggregate, (ii) prediction errors computed on common available records, and (iii) qualification and structural diagnostics. In the reported aggregate, the two data sources receive equal mass, cases are equally weighted within source, and seeds and budgets are equally weighted. Missing predictions receive worst-case defaults: MAE and RMSE are set to one, and rank correlations to minus one. Absent baseline records similarly receive MAE one. These defaults are operational penalties, not measured model predictions.\npaper.tex:141:\\section{Results}\npaper.tex:142:\\subsection{Completed Evaluation, Failed Qualification}\npaper.tex:143:The final evaluation completed and failed qualification. Table~\\ref{tab:aggregate} preserves its scored summaries. Primary MAE is 0.0880 and RMSE is 0.0979; reported Spearman and Kendall correlations are 0.7329 and 0.5943. These summaries include defaults for missing primary records. Their ordering against the fitted uniform-selection MAE of 0.0449 cannot be interpreted as a measured overall selection disadvantage.\npaper.tex:191:\\subsection{Conditional Comparison Across Fixed Cases}\npaper.tex:198:\\caption{Available-pair MAE, averaged equally over query budgets, separated by data source. Small dots show supported seeds; vertical marks show their descriptive mean. Parentheses give supported seed counts; the asterisk marks missing Commonsense records at seeds 5003 and 9001. All four methods use identical case--seed support. Missing-output penalties are not plotted as measured errors. These conditional results do not restore failed qualification.}\npaper.tex:205:\\subsection{Item Budget, Reconstruction, and Ranking}\npaper.tex:221:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\npaper.tex:234:\\section{Conclusion}\npaper.tex:235:Score-targeted fixed prefixes and source-trained reconstruction provide a concrete approach to few-item benchmark prediction. The retained evaluation supports heterogeneous conditional accuracy comparisons and shows that fitted reconstruction can change score error more than rank ordering on the same selected responses. However, the final assessment completed without qualification: missing primary and reference predictions and failed matched structural checks are essential parts of the result, not peripheral implementation notes.\npaper.tex:243:\\section*{LLM Usage}\npaper.tex:244:LLM-based agents contributed to method formulation, estimator implementation, literature-query planning, evidence transformation code, and manuscript drafting. The writing step inspected the supplied estimator and evaluator, curated references using exact bibliographic identifiers, and generated descriptive summaries from frozen predictions. It did not generate target answers, fabricate experiments, rerun the final assessment, or use model-generated numerical outputs as independent evidence. Automated checks support artifact consistency; they do not substitute for scientific qualification, independent replication, or human review.\npaper.tex:248:\\section{Supplementary Information}\npaper.tex:249:\\subsection{Inspectable Implementation and Reproducibility}\npaper.tex:280:\\subsection{Extended Estimation Details}\npaper.tex:289:\\subsection{Local Reference Implementations}\npaper.tex:296:\\subsection{Available-Pair Numerical Results}\npaper.tex:353:\\subsection{Seed-Level Records and Exhaustive Numerical Ledger}\npaper.tex:360:\\caption{All primary case--seed records. $T$ counts targets and $H$ their recorded groups. Errors and correlations average the three budgets. Missing outputs are not measured errors; available rows do not repair failed overall qualification.}\npaper.tex:413:\\subsection{Availability Masks and Statistical Interpretation}\npaper.tex:417:The seed dots in the budget figure separately renormalize the original case weights over cases available for each seed. Consequently their average need not equal the large pooled dot, whose normalization is within each case. They convey descriptive variation under overlapping model-group partitions and should not be used as independent replicates for a significance test. No new bootstrap or inferential procedure is introduced by the paper's plotting code. All qualification facts remain those of the frozen evaluator.\nreferences.bib:1:@misc{Vivek2024,\nreferences.bib:9:@misc{Polo2024,\nreferences.bib:17:@misc{Kipnis2025,\nreferences.bib:25:@misc{Pacchiardi2024,\nreferences.bib:33:@misc{Li2025,\nreferences.bib:41:@misc{Liang2023,\nreferences.bib:49:@misc{Wang2024,\nreferences.bib:57:@misc{Hoerl1970,\nreferences.bib:65:@misc{Elfving1952,\nreferences.bib:73:@misc{Kiefer1960,\nreferences.bib:81:@misc{Andersen1970,\nreferences.bib:89:@misc{Dempster1977,\nreferences.bib:97:@misc{Hastie2009,\nreferences.bib:105:@misc{Bishop2006,\nreferences.bib:113:@misc{Efron1979,\nreferences.bib:121:@misc{Rubin1976,\nreferences.bib:129:@misc{Little2019,\nreferences.bib:137:@misc{Roberts2017,\nreferences.bib:145:@misc{Varma2006,\nreferences.bib:153:@misc{Arlot2010,\nreferences.bib:161:@misc{Stone1974,\nreferences.bib:169:@misc{Kendall1938,\nreferences.bib:177:@misc{Spearman1904,\nreferences.bib:185:@misc{BenDavid2010,\nreferences.bib:193:@misc{Brier1950,\nls: cannot access '.codex/skills/{validate-paper,write-paper-standalone,scientific-evidence,memory-log}/scripts': No such file or directory\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent 16 Nov 13 18:01 global_memory -> ../investigation\n{\n  \"figure_schema\": \"passed\",\n  \"json_pointers_checked\": 48,\n  \"available_pair_recalculation\": \"passed\",\n  \"figures_referenced\": \"passed\",\n  \"body_pages\": 8,\n  \"last_body_geometric_fill\": 0.8964284261067709,\n  \"supplementary_start_page\": 11,\n  \"standalone_language\": \"passed\",\n  \"resolved_recorder_inputs_checked\": 122,\n  \"note\": \"Local checks. Orchestrator-owned diagram is a placeholder pending rendering. Canonical figure validator cannot import src.orchestrator in this workspace; canonical recorder rejects a standard TeX configuration symlink across two approved roots.\"\n}---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [##----------] | refs 5/25 (20 left) | candidates 5/25 | query 2/3\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [###---------] | refs 6/25 (19 left) | candidates 6/25 | query 2/