{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"ls source_evidence; cat source_evidence/program_spec.toml; sed -n '1,80p' source_evidence/selected_method.py; sed -n '103,192p' source_evidence/selected_method.py; sed -n '732,773p' source_evidence/evaluate_final.py; cat draft_reference_gaps.json; cat postdraft_reference_query_plan.json\"","aggregated_output":"METHOD.md\nREADME.md\nevaluate_final.py\nfinal_data_manifest.json\nprogram_spec.toml\nselected_method.py\nsnapshot_manifest.json\nschema_version = 1\ncode_root = \"src\"\n\n[entrypoint]\npath = \"program.py\"\nlanguage = \"python\"\ntype = \"script\"\n\n[copy]\nexclude = [\n  \"**/__pycache__/**\",\n  \"**/*.pyc\",\n  \".venv/**\",\n  \"node_modules/**\",\n  \".git/**\",\n  \".mypy_cache/**\",\n  \".pytest_cache/**\",\n  \".ruff_cache/**\",\n  \".tox/**\",\n  \".nox/**\",\n  \"dist/**\",\n  \"build/**\",\n]\ninclude = [\n]\n\n[integration]\nkind = \"generic\"\nnotes = \"\"\n\"\"\"Score-targeted prefixes with group-balanced Rasch-residual mixtures.\n\nFinite JSON RPC, handle(request)->dict; extra response fields are forbidden.\nEvery episode: reset, source_chunk (1..2 calls), fit, observe (8,16,32), release.\nNo files, target IDs, benchmark identities, or private target scores are provided.\nSeeds initialize randomness only; arbitrary nonnegative integer seeds are valid.\n\nreset: {op:'reset',seed:int} -> {ok:true}. Clears all buffers and fitted state.\nsource_chunk: {op,encoding:'base64',dtype:'uint8',shape:[S,N],offset:int,\ntotal_bytes:S*N,chunk_count:int,data:str} -> {ok:true}. C-order contiguous bytes,\nin order, at most two chunks, each <=4194304 decoded bytes; S<=512,N<=16384.\nThe first chunk allocates candidate-owned RAM; no filesystem exchange exists.\nfit: {op,mode:'primary'|'no_diversity'|'no_calibration',budgets:[8,16,32],\nsource_groups:[int]*S} -> {anchors:[int]*32}. Requires complete source bytes.\nAnchors are distinct indices in [0,N), ordered nested prefixes for each budget.\nOpaque source groups permit source-only grouped CV; target data never calibrates.\nobserve: {op,budget:int,encoding:'base64',dtype:'uint8',shape:[T,budget],\ndata:str} -> {encoding:'base64',dtype:'<f8',shape:[T],data:str}.\nInput contains ONLY cumulative queried target correctness bits, C-order. Each\ncheckpoint supplies the complete legal prefix (not additional target queries).\nPrediction bytes are finite little-endian float64 probabilities in [0,1].\nTargets have no persistent identities: identical queried rows must receive equal\npredictions. Evaluator may supply matched constant/copied/permuted observations.\nNo response flag or internal-state claim contributes to scientific validity.\nrelease: {op:'release'} -> {ok:true}. Releases source and fit RAM. No other ops.\n\nThe editable directory may contain method/paper sources. Research conclusions,\nPDF/template compliance, genuine ablation interpretation and novelty require\nhuman-readable evidence beyond these operational prediction metrics. Search is\ndevelopment-only: no comparative baseline threshold is an eligibility gate.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport base64\nimport numpy as np\n\nSTATE = {}\n\n\ndef expit(values):\n    return 1.0 / (1.0 + np.exp(-values))\n\n\ndef logit(values):\n    return np.log(values) - np.log1p(-values)\n\n\ndef logsumexp(values, axis=None, keepdims=False):\n    maximum = np.max(values, axis=axis, keepdims=True)\n    result = maximum + np.log(np.exp(values - maximum).sum(axis=axis, keepdims=True))\n    if keepdims:\n        return result\n    return np.squeeze(result, axis=axis)\n\n\ndef group_weights(source_groups):\n    unique, group_inverse, group_counts = np.unique(source_groups, return_inverse=True, return_counts=True)\n    return 1.0 / (len(unique) * group_counts[group_inverse])\n\n\ndef select_anchors(source, source_groups, seed, count, uniform=False):\n    random = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    if uniform:\n        query_order = random.permutation(source.shape[1])[:count]\n    else:\n        weights = group_weights(source_groups)\n        source_scores = source.astype(np.float64)\n        item_mean = weights @ source_scores\n        full_scores = source_scores.mean(axis=1)\n        centered = (source_scores - item_mean) * np.sqrt(weights[:, None])\n        score_centered = (full_scores - weights @ full_scores) * np.sqrt(weights)\n        score_covariance = centered.T @ score_centered\n        noise = 0.1 * np.maximum(item_mean * (1.0 - item_mean), 0.05)\n        conditional_variance = np.sum(centered * centered, axis=0) + noise\n        factors = np.zeros((source.shape[1], count), dtype=np.float64)\n        selected = np.zeros(source.shape[1], dtype=bool)\n        query_order = np.empty(count, dtype=np.int64)\n        for step in range(count):\n            gains = score_covariance ** 2 / np.maximum(conditional_variance, 1e-12)\n            gains[selected] = -np.inf\n            current = int(np.argmax(gains))\n\ndef fit_rasch(source_scores, source_groups):\n    weights = group_weights(source_groups)\n    source_ability = logit((source_scores.sum(axis=1) + 0.5) / (source_scores.shape[1] + 1.0))\n    item_mean = np.clip(weights @ source_scores, 1e-4, 1.0 - 1e-4)\n    item_difficulty = weights @ source_ability - logit(item_mean)\n    for iteration in range(8):\n        probabilities = expit(source_ability[:, None] - item_difficulty[None, :])\n        gradient = weights @ (source_scores - probabilities) + 0.01 * item_difficulty\n        curvature = weights @ (probabilities * (1.0 - probabilities)) + 0.01\n        item_difficulty -= np.clip(gradient / curvature, -2.0, 2.0)\n    rasch_probs = expit(source_ability[:, None] - item_difficulty[None, :])\n    return rasch_probs\n\n\ndef make_profile(source_scores, rasch_probs, residual_weight):\n    probability_floor = 1e-6\n    profile_probs = np.clip((1.0 - residual_weight) * rasch_probs + residual_weight * source_scores, probability_floor, 1.0 - probability_floor)\n    return profile_probs\n\n\ndef particle_prior(source_groups):\n    unique, group_inverse, group_counts = np.unique(source_groups, return_inverse=True, return_counts=True)\n    n_groups = len(unique)\n    source_log_prior = -np.log(n_groups) - np.log(group_counts[group_inverse])\n    offset_log_prior = np.log(np.array([1.0, 4.0, 6.0, 4.0, 1.0]) / 16.0)\n    particle_log_prior = (source_log_prior[:, None] + offset_log_prior[None, :]).reshape(-1)\n    assert np.isclose(np.exp(particle_log_prior).sum(), 1.0)\n    group_mass = np.bincount(group_inverse, weights=np.exp(source_log_prior))\n    assert np.allclose(group_mass, 1.0 / n_groups)\n    return particle_log_prior\n\n\ndef make_particles(profile_probs, offset_scale):\n    n_items = profile_probs.shape[1]\n    ability_offsets = offset_scale * np.array([-2.0, -1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 2.0])\n    particle_probs = expit(logit(profile_probs)[:, None, :] + ability_offsets[None, :, None]).reshape(-1, n_items)\n    return particle_probs\n\n\ndef particle_summary(profile_probs, offset_scale, query_order):\n    ability_offsets = offset_scale * np.array([-2.0, -1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 2.0])\n    n_rows, n_items = profile_probs.shape\n    selected_probs = expit(logit(profile_probs[:, query_order])[:, None, :] + ability_offsets[None, :, None]).reshape(-1, len(query_order))\n    if offset_scale == 0.0:\n        totals = np.repeat(profile_probs.sum(axis=1), 5)\n    else:\n        totals = np.zeros(n_rows * 5, dtype=np.float64)\n        for start in range(0, n_items, 512):\n            block = logit(profile_probs[:, start:start + 512])\n            probabilities = expit(block[:, None, :] + ability_offsets[None, :, None])\n            totals += probabilities.sum(axis=2).reshape(-1)\n    return selected_probs, totals\n\n\ndef predict_summary(observed_probs, particle_log_prior, totals, n_items, observations):\n    log_failure = np.log1p(-observed_probs)\n    log_success = np.log(observed_probs)\n    log_posterior = observations @ (log_success - log_failure).T\n    log_posterior += log_failure.sum(axis=1)[None, :] + particle_log_prior[None, :]\n    posterior_weights = np.exp(log_posterior - logsumexp(log_posterior, axis=1, keepdims=True))\n    assert np.allclose(posterior_weights.sum(axis=1), 1.0)\n    remaining_totals = totals - observed_probs.sum(axis=1)\n    predictions = (observations.sum(axis=1) + posterior_weights @ remaining_totals) / n_items\n    assert np.isfinite(predictions).all()\n    return np.clip(predictions, 0.0, 1.0)\n\n\ndef predict_particles(particle_probs, particle_log_prior, totals, observed_indices, observations):\n    assert np.unique(observed_indices).size == observed_indices.size\n    n_items = particle_probs.shape[1]\n    observed_probs = particle_probs[:, observed_indices]\n    if len(observations) != 1:\n        return predict_summary(observed_probs, particle_log_prior, totals, n_items, observations)\n    observed_answers = observations[0]\n    log_likelihood = (observed_answers[None, :] * np.log(observed_probs) + (1.0 - observed_answers[None, :]) * np.log1p(-observed_probs)).sum(axis=1)\n    log_posterior = particle_log_prior + log_likelihood\n    posterior_weights = np.exp(log_posterior - logsumexp(log_posterior))\n    assert np.isclose(posterior_weights.sum(), 1.0)\n    remaining_mask = np.ones(n_items, dtype=bool)\n    remaining_mask[observed_indices] = False\n    particle_prediction = (observed_answers.sum() + posterior_weights @ particle_probs[:, remaining_mask].sum(axis=1)) / n_items\n    assert np.isfinite(particle_prediction)\n    return np.clip(np.array([particle_prediction]), 0.0, 1.0)\n\n\ndef linear_predict(model, observations):\n    center, mean, coefficient, shrinkage = model\n    return np.clip(mean + shrinkage * ((observations - center) @ coefficient), 0.0, 1.0)\n\ndef irt_baseline(source, target):\n    import numpy as np\n    from scipy.optimize import minimize\n    from scipy.special import expit, logit\n    source = source.astype(float)\n    rows, columns = source.shape\n    initial_theta = logit(np.clip(source.mean(axis=1), .02, .98))\n    initial_theta = (initial_theta - initial_theta.mean()) / max(initial_theta.std(), .1)\n    initial = np.concatenate((initial_theta, np.zeros(columns), -logit(np.clip(source.mean(axis=0), .02, .98))))\n\n    def objective(parameters):\n        theta = parameters[:rows]\n        log_discrimination = parameters[rows:rows + columns]\n        discrimination = np.exp(log_discrimination)\n        difficulty = parameters[rows + columns:]\n        logits = theta[:, None] * discrimination - difficulty\n        residual = expit(logits) - source\n        loss = np.sum(np.logaddexp(0, logits) - source * logits) + .5 * np.sum(theta ** 2) + .05 * np.sum(log_discrimination ** 2) + .005 * np.sum(difficulty ** 2)\n        gradient = np.concatenate((residual @ discrimination + theta, np.sum(residual * theta[:, None], axis=0) * discrimination + .1 * log_discrimination, -residual.sum(axis=0) + .01 * difficulty))\n        return float(loss), gradient\n\n    fitted = minimize(objective, initial, method='L-BFGS-B', jac=True, bounds=[(-6, 6)] * rows + [(-3, 2)] * columns + [(-8, 8)] * columns, options={'maxiter': 40, 'maxls': 20, 'ftol': 1e-7})\n    if not np.isfinite(fitted.x).all() or fitted.fun > objective(initial)[0] + 1e-7:\n        raise RuntimeError('invalid IRT solver result')\n    discrimination = np.exp(fitted.x[rows:rows + columns])\n    difficulty = fitted.x[rows + columns:]\n    probability = expit(-difficulty)\n    order = np.argsort(-(discrimination ** 2 * probability * (1 - probability)), kind='stable')[:32]\n    predictions = []\n    for budget in BUDGETS:\n        selected = order[:budget]\n        answers = target[:, selected]\n        ability = np.zeros(len(target))\n        for step in range(30):\n            probability = expit(ability[:, None] * discrimination[selected] - difficulty[selected])\n            gradient = ((probability - answers) * discrimination[selected]).sum(axis=1) + ability\n            hessian = (probability * (1 - probability) * discrimination[selected] ** 2).sum(axis=1) + 1\n            ability = np.clip(ability - np.clip(gradient / hessian, -1, 1), -6, 6)\n        predictions.append(expit(ability[:, None] * discrimination - difficulty).mean(axis=1))\n    return np.asarray(predictions)\n\n\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"summary\": \"Two paragraph-specific gaps justify a small retrieval pass for Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark context and direct Rasch/IRT foundations; the existing related-work coverage needs no expansion.\",\n  \"missing_literatures\": [\n    \"Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark collection provenance\",\n    \"Rasch and two-parameter logistic item-response model foundations\"\n  ],\n  \"rewrite_targets\": [\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"evaluation-leaderboard-context\",\n      \"section\": \"Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability\",\n      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"reason\": \"The opening benchmark paragraph names four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases without a direct reference to that collection, and the bibliography contains no dedicated Leaderboard source. Liang2023 already supplies HELM context and can be reused locally without retrieval. One authoritative Leaderboard collection reference would identify the upstream evaluation context; it must not be used to substantiate the staged case counts, exact snapshot, filtering, or benchmark-version equivalence, which remain artifact-grounded.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math)\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark collection documentation BBH GPQA MuSR OpenLLM Math\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"method-rasch-irt-foundations\",\n      \"section\": \"Method\",\n      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"background\",\n      \"reason\": \"The reconstruction paragraph introduces a Rasch response model without a direct foundational citation. Related Work cites Andersen1970 specifically to distinguish conditional maximum-likelihood estimation, not to explain the response-model family; its stored abstract concerns conditional estimation. The later local comparator uses a two-parameter logistic model and fitted item information, also without a direct foundational reference. One or two authoritative IRT sources could support these named model families and their distinction without implying that the custom fitting procedure is standard, that the local comparator reproduces a published system, or that the profile branch has an established empirical benefit.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"The nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model.\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"Rasch logistic item-response model ability item difficulty foundational reference\",\n        \"two-parameter logistic item-response model discrimination Fisher item information authoritative reference\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Evidence-bounded prediction of LLM full binary benchmark scores and rankings from fixed 8-, 16-, or 32-item prefixes using source-only score-covariance selection and ridge/Rasch-residual reconstruction; incremental citation support is needed only for upstream Open LLM Leaderboard collection context and Rasch/2PL foundations.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Source-only score-targeted fixed nested prefixes with grouped tuning and ridge score reconstruction; existing methodological citations require no new search\",\n    \"Rasch ability-minus-difficulty response probabilities used to smooth source profiles; direct model-family foundations are missing at paper.tex:86\",\n    \"Fixed-ability, group-weighted penalized fitting and optional residual-profile blending remain artifact-grounded custom implementation details, not claims licensed by a foundational citation\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"The local penalized two-parameter logistic comparator uses item discrimination and fitted Fisher item information; foundational support is needed at paper.tex:130 and paper.tex:293\",\n    \"Anchor Points, tinyBenchmarks, metabench, reference-instance prediction, and active evaluation acquisition already have direct citations; no comparator-family expansion is needed\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"Open LLM Leaderboard-derived final cases BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math need one authoritative upstream collection reference at paper.tex:115; staged identities, counts, filtering, and snapshot details remain artifact-grounded\",\n    \"HELM-derived final cases Commonsense, GSM, LegalBench, MATH, MedQA, and MMLU need no new retrieval; reuse Liang2023 for HELM collection context, and do not use Wang2024 (MMLU-Pro) as provenance for staged MMLU\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"Completed but unqualified evaluation, missing predictions, structural diagnostics, and retained-support results are artifact-grounded; additional references cannot repair or explain them\",\n    \"No citation enrichment should imply selector superiority, an isolated residual-profile benefit, calibrated target uncertainty, or official reproduction of a published comparator\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark collection documentation BBH GPQA MuSR OpenLLM Math\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Fills evaluation-leaderboard-context at the opening paragraph of Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability / Fixed Benchmarks and Grouped Model Holdouts (paper.tex:115). Seek one authoritative collection source documenting the upstream benchmark composition. The initial plan already targeted Leaderboard context, but the 25-entry bibliography and provenance contain no dedicated collection source, so this is an unresolved gap rather than a literature refresh. Support only the named collection context, not staged case counts, filtering, snapshot identity, or equivalence with public benchmark versions. Reuse Liang2023 for HELM without another query; Wang2024 concerns MMLU-Pro, not staged MMLU.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Rasch logistic item-response model ability item difficulty foundational reference\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Fills method-rasch-irt-foundations at Method / Linear and Residual-Profile Reconstruction, specifically 'The nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model.' (paper.tex:86). Seek an authoritative foundation for the logistic ability-minus-difficulty response family, preferably also explaining the 2PL distinction and item information so the source can be reused for the local comparator. Andersen1970 is already cited specifically for conditional maximum-likelihood estimation, which the draft does not use; its stored abstract does not directly close this model-family gap. Do not characterize the fixed-ability penalized custom fit as standard joint Rasch estimation or infer empirical profile benefit.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"two-parameter logistic item-response model discrimination Fisher item information authoritative reference\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Conditional complement within method-rasch-irt-foundations: execute only if the Rasch source does not also support the local 2PL comparator in Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability / Comparators and Control Semantics (paper.tex:130) and Supplementary Information / Local Reference Implementations (paper.tex:293). Seek at most one complementary authoritative source for variable discrimination and Fisher information a^2 p(1-p), checking consistency with the draft's theta*a-d parameterization. This is model-family framing, not a new comparator search or evidence that the local penalized fit reproduces tinyBenchmarks or another published package.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"Broad LLM benchmark-compression or comparator-family refreshes, citation-graph expansion, citing/cited-by traversal, or related-paper recommendations; existing named comparator coverage is sufficient\",\n    \"Any attempt to identify, infer, search for, or access the task-prohibited unnamed paper or its authors, title, findings, mirrors, summaries, reviews, presentations, repositories, or discussions, as restricted by the existing reference_query_plan.json\",\n    \"Separate searches for all ten benchmark cases, another HELM collection search, or reuse of MMLU-Pro provenance for staged MMLU\",\n    \"Treating an upstream collection reference as evidence for staged counts, filtering, snapshot identity, fixed item universes, or benchmark-version equivalence\",\n    \"Latent-class or residual-mixture literature expansion, new acquisition methods, deployment speedups, new benchmark populations, target-adaptive policies, calibrated stopping rules, or architecture-transfer claims\",\n    \"Using foundational IRT sources to certify custom optimizer correctness, official comparator reproduction, target-calibrated uncertainty, profile-component benefit, selector superiority, or a cause of failed qualification\",\n    \"Retrieving references during this planning stage; the third query is conditional and later retrieval should stop after approximately two to three unique sources across the two documented gaps\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
