{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '286,303p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,65p' .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts/validate_claims.py; sed -n '65,150p' .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts/validate_claims.py; cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json | head -85; tail -22 reference_consultation.md; sed -n '1,80p' retrieve_vetted_references.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '286,303p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,65p' .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts/validate_claims.py; sed -n '65,150p' .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts/validate_claims.py; cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json | head -85; tail -22 reference_consultation.md; sed -n '1,80p' retrieve_vetted_references.py\"","aggregated_output":"\nEquation~\\ref{eq:profile} explicitly separates known and predicted items. In the batch path, the expected unqueried total is computed as each particle's full expected total minus its queried expected total. The single-row path sums over an explicit unqueried mask. Both implement the same algebra. This source inspection supports an implementation description, not an empirical guarantee that every execution path passed the evaluator's controls. The final diagnostic failures remain in force.\n\n\\subsection{Local Reference Implementations}\nThe anchor reference centers each item's source response vector, normalizes its length, and applies $k$-means with $k=b$, one initialization, and at most 30 iterations. Representatives are distinct nearest response vectors. Every item is reassigned to its nearest representative and representative weights are the fractions assigned. Target score prediction is the weighted average of queried responses. This reference need not match the preprocessing, optimization, or benchmark regime of published Anchor Points.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L711-L729]]\n\nThe IRT reference models logits as $\\theta_i\\exp(\\eta_j)-d_j$. Its objective is summed Bernoulli negative log likelihood plus penalties $0.5\\|\\theta\\|^2+0.05\\|\\eta\\|^2+0.005\\|d\\|^2$. L-BFGS-B uses bounded parameters and at most 40 iterations. Item order is the decreasing fitted information $a_j^2 p_j(1-p_j)$ at zero ability, where $a_j=\\exp(\\eta_j)$. Target abilities use up to 30 regularized Newton updates on queried responses; the reference score averages fitted response probabilities over the entire item universe. Finite-objective checks do not prove solver optimality. No exact reproduction of a published IRT benchmark-compression package is claimed.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L732-L771]]\n\n\\subsection{Available-Pair Numerical Results}\nTable~\\ref{tab:cases_si} retains exact case-level descriptive means to more precision than the main text. All methods within a row use the same supported seeds and give equal weight to each budget and target within a seed. The table is not an extrapolation to absent records. In particular, the Commonsense row is a single split, and cannot quantify its between-seed stability.\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Conditional MAE on common available support. $n$ counts supported seeds.}\n\\label{tab:cases_si}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Validate generated claim-grounding artifacts before paper finalization.\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport json\nimport os\nimport re\nimport stat\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport tempfile\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any\n\nKEY_RE = re.compile(r\"@\\w+\\{([^,]+),\")\nEVIDENCE_REF_RE = re.compile(r\"@evidence\\[\\[[^\\]]+\\]\\]\")\nTEX_INPUT_RE = re.compile(\n    r\"\\\\(?:input|include)(?![A-Za-z@])\\s*\"\n    r\"(?:\\{(?P<braced>[^{}]+)\\}|(?P<unbraced>[^\\s%{}]+))\"\n)\nTEX_IMPORT_COMMAND_RE = re.compile(r\"\\\\(?:input|include)(?![A-Za-z@])\")\nTEX_LOCAL_SUPPORT_RE = re.compile(\n    r\"\\\\(?P<command>usepackage|RequirePackage|documentclass|LoadClass)\"\n    r\"(?![A-Za-z@])(?:\\s*\\[[^\\]]*\\])?\\s*\\{(?P<targets>[^{}]+)\\}\"\n)\nTEX_LOCAL_SUPPORT_COMMAND_RE = re.compile(\n    r\"\\\\(?:usepackage|RequirePackage|documentclass|LoadClass)(?![A-Za-z@])\"\n)\nDYNAMIC_TEX_IMPORT_RE = re.compile(r\"\\\\csname\\s*(?:input|include)\\s*\\\\endcsname\")\nCONDITIONAL_TOKEN_RE = re.compile(r\"\\\\if[a-zA-Z@]+\\b|\\\\else\\b|\\\\fi\\b\")\nVERBATIM_ENV_RE = re.compile(\n    r\"\\\\begin\\s*\\{(?P<env>verbatim\\*?|lstlisting|minted)\\}.*?\"\n    r\"\\\\end\\s*\\{(?P=env)\\}\",\n    re.DOTALL,\n)\nPROMPT_ARTIFACT_RE = re.compile(r\".+_prompt(?:_round_\\d+(?:_contract_retry_\\d+)?)?\\.md$\")\nGENERATED_EVIDENCE_SCAN_ARTIFACT_NAMES = {\n    \"validation_feedback.json\",\n    \"validation_feedback.md\",\n    \"verification-report.json\",\n}\nROOT_GENERATED_EVIDENCE_SCAN_ARTIFACT_NAMES = {\"validation_report.json\"}\nGENERATED_EVIDENCE_SCAN_ARTIFACT_PREFIXES = (\n    \"paper_evidence_audit\",\n)\nEVIDENCE_BUNDLE_DIRNAME = \"scientific-evidence\"\nEVIDENCE_SCAN_SUFFIXES = (\n    \".tex\",\n    \".md\",\n    \".json\",\n    \".yaml\",\n    \".yml\",\n    \".py\",\n    \".sh\",\n    \".r\",\n    \".csv\",\n    \".tsv\",\n)\nEVIDENCE_SKIP_DIR_NAMES = {\n    \".git\",\n    \"__pycache__\",\n    EVIDENCE_BUNDLE_DIRNAME,\n}\n}\nAGENT_SKILL_DIR_PREFIXES = (\n    (\".claude\", \"skills\"),\n    (\".codex\", \"skills\"),\n    (\".cursor\", \"skills\"),\n    (\".opencode\", \"skills\"),\n    (\".gemini\", \"skills\"),\n    (\".pi\", \"agent\", \"skills\"),\n)\nVERIFICATION_SUMMARY_KEYS = (\n    \"total_references\",\n    \"resolved_references\",\n    \"anchored_references\",\n    \"dangling_references\",\n    \"manifests_checked\",\n)\nSCIENTIFIC_EVIDENCE_SCRIPT = (\n    Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent.parent\n    / \"scientific-evidence\"\n    / \"scripts\"\n    / \"scientific_evidence.py\"\n)\n\n\ndef read_json(path: Path) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Read JSON data from disk when available.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        payload = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n    except json.JSONDecodeError as exc:\n        raise SystemExit(f\"Invalid JSON in {path}: {exc}\") from exc\n    if not isinstance(payload, dict):\n        raise SystemExit(f\"Expected JSON object in {path}\")\n    return payload\n\n\ndef read_bib_keys(path: Path) -> set[str]:\n    \"\"\"Read bib keys.\"\"\"\n    text = path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    return {match.group(1).strip() for match in KEY_RE.finditer(text) if match.group(1).strip()}\n\n\ndef validate_reference_provenance(*, references_payload: dict[str, Any], bib_keys: set[str]) -> tuple[set[str], list[str]]:\n    \"\"\"Validate references_provenance.json against references.bib.\"\"\"\n    errors: list[str] = []\n    refs = references_payload.get(\"references\")\n    if not isinstance(refs, list):\n        return set(), [\"references_provenance.json must contain a `references` list.\"]\n\n    provenance_keys: set[str] = set()\n    provenance_ids: set[str] = set()\n    for idx, row in enumerate(refs):\n        if not isinstance(row, dict):\n            errors.append(f\"references[{idx}] must be object\")\n            continue\n        source_id = row.get(\"id\")\n        if not isinstance(source_id, str) or not source_id.strip():\n            errors.append(f\"references[{idx}].id missing\")\n        else:\n            if source_id in provenance_ids:\n                errors.append(f\"references[{idx}].id duplicates existing id: {source_id!r}\")\n            provenance_ids.add(source_id)\n\n        citation_key = row.get(\"citation_key\")\n        if not isinstance(citation_key, str) or not citation_key.strip():\n            errors.append(f\"references[{idx}].citation_key missing\")\n            continue\n        if citation_key in provenance_keys:\n            errors.append(\n                f\"references[{idx}].citation_key duplicates existing key: {citation_key!r}\"\n            )\n        provenance_keys.add(citation_key)\n\n    missing_in_provenance = sorted(bib_keys - provenance_keys)\n    if missing_in_provenance:\n        errors.append(\n            \"references.bib keys missing from references_provenance.json: \"\n            + \", \".join(repr(key) for key in missing_in_provenance)\n        )\n\n    missing_in_bib = sorted(provenance_keys - bib_keys)\n    if missing_in_bib:\n        errors.append(\n            \"references_provenance.json keys missing from references.bib: \"\n            + \", \".join(repr(key) for key in missing_in_bib)\n        )\n\ncat: .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json: No such file or directory\n\nSummary A generalized form of the cross-validation criterion is applied to the choice and assessment of prediction using the data-analytic concept of a prescription. The examples used to illustrate the application are drawn from the problem areas of univariate estimation, linear regression and analysis of variance.\n\n## Kendall1938: A NEW MEASURE OF RANK CORRELATION\nhttps://api.openalex.org/works/https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/30.1-2.81\n\nNo abstract returned. Metadata only; use only for bibliographic context.\n\n## Spearman1904: The Proof and Measurement of Association between Two Things\nhttps://api.openalex.org/works/https://doi.org/10.2307/1412159\n\nNo abstract returned. Metadata only; use only for bibliographic context.\n\n## BenDavid2010: A theory of learning from different domains\nhttps://api.openalex.org/works/https://doi.org/10.1007/s10994-009-5152-4\n\nDiscriminative learning methods for classification perform well when training and test data are drawn from the same distribution. Often, however, we have plentiful labeled training data from a source domain but wish to learn a classifier which performs well on a target domain with a different distribution and little or no labeled training data. In this work we investigate two questions. First, under what conditions can a classifier trained from source data be expected to perform well on target data? Second, given a small amount of labeled target data, how should we combine it during training with the large amount of labeled source data to achieve the lowest target error at test time? We address the first question by bounding a classifier’s target error in terms of its source error and the divergence between the two domains. We give a classifier-induced divergence measure that can be estimated from finite, unlabeled samples from the domains. Under the assumption that there exists some hypothesis that performs well in both domains, we show that this quantity together with the empirical source error characterize the target error of a source-trained classifier. We answer the second question by bounding the target error of a model which minimizes a convex combination of the empirical source and target errors. Previous theoretical work has considered minimizing just the source error, just the target error, or weighting instances from the two domains equally. We show how to choose the optimal combination of source and target error as a function of the divergence, the sample sizes of both domains, and the complexity of the hypothesis class. The resulting bound generalizes the previously studied cases and is always at least as tight as a bound which considers minimizing only the target error or an equal weighting of source and target errors.\n\n## Brier1950: VERIFICATION OF FORECASTS EXPRESSED IN TERMS OF PROBABILITY\nhttps://api.openalex.org/works/https://doi.org/10.1175/1520-0493(1950)078<0001:VOFEIT>2.0.CO;2\n\nNo abstract returned. Metadata only; use only for bibliographic context.import concurrent.futures\nimport datetime\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport requests\n\nTARGETS = [\n    ('Vivek2024', '10.48550/arXiv.2309.08638'),\n    ('Polo2024', '10.48550/arXiv.2402.14992'),\n    ('Kipnis2025', '10.48550/arXiv.2407.12844'),\n    ('Pacchiardi2024', '10.48550/arXiv.2409.03563'),\n    ('Li2025', '10.48550/arXiv.2410.05952'),\n    ('Liang2023', '10.48550/arXiv.2211.09110'),\n    ('Wang2024', '10.48550/arXiv.2406.01574'),\n    ('Hoerl1970', '10.1080/00401706.1970.10488634'),\n    ('Elfving1952', '10.1214/aoms/1177729442'),\n    ('Kiefer1960', '10.4153/CJM-1960-030-4'),\n    ('Andersen1970', '10.1111/j.2517-6161.1970.tb00842.x'),\n    ('Dempster1977', '10.1111/j.2517-6161.1977.tb01600.x'),\n    ('Hastie2009', '10.1007/978-0-387-84858-7'),\n    ('Bishop2006', '10.1007/978-0-387-45528-0'),\n    ('Efron1979', '10.1214/aos/1176344552'),\n    ('Rubin1976', '10.1093/biomet/63.3.581'),\n    ('Little2019', '10.1002/9781119013563'),\n    ('Roberts2017', '10.1111/ecog.02881'),\n    ('Varma2006', '10.1186/1471-2105-7-91'),\n    ('Arlot2010', '10.1214/09-SS054'),\n    ('Stone1974', '10.1111/j.2517-6161.1974.tb00994.x'),\n    ('Kendall1938', '10.1093/biomet/30.1-2.81'),\n    ('Spearman1904', '10.2307/1412159'),\n    ('BenDavid2010', '10.1007/s10994-009-5152-4'),\n    ('Brier1950', '10.1175/1520-0493(1950)078<0001:VOFEIT>2.0.CO;2'),\n]\n\n\ndef fetch(target):\n    key, doi = target\n    url = 'https://api.openalex.org/works/https://doi.org/' + doi\n    response = requests.get(url, timeout=60)\n    response.raise_for_status()\n    work = response.json()\n    index = work.get('abstract_inverted_index') or {}\n    positions = {position: word for word, locations in index.items() for position in locations}\n    abstract = ' '.join(positions[position] for position in sorted(positions))\n    authors = ' and '.join(author['author']['display_name'] for author in work['authorships'])\n    title = work['title'].replace('&', r'\\&')\n    venue = ((work.get('primary_location') or {}).get('source') or {}).get('display_name') or 'Preprint'\n    bib = f'@misc{{{key},\\n title = {{{title}}},\\n author = {{{authors}}},\\n year = {{{work[\"publication_year\"]}}},\\n howpublished = {{{venue}}},\\n doi = {{{doi}}}\\n}}\\n'\n    record = dict(id='ref.openalex.' + work['id'].split('/')[-1], paper_id=work['id'], citation_key=key,\n                  title=work['title'], year=work['publication_year'], venue=venue, query='Exact DOI: ' + doi,\n                  source_url=url, retrieved_at=datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat(),\n                  abstract=abstract, doi=doi, consulted_scope='OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed')\n    return bib, record\n\n\ndef main():\n    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=4) as executor:\n        results = list(executor.map(fetch, TARGETS))\n    Path('references.bib').write_text('\\n'.join(result[0] for result in results))\n    Path('references_provenance.json').write_text(json.dumps(dict(\n        generated_at=datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat(), count=len(results),\n        queries=['Exact DOI: ' + doi for _, doi in TARGETS], references=[result[1] for result in results],\n        retrieval_note='Semantic Scholar exact-ID requests returned HTTP 429. OpenAlex exact-DOI fallback. Replaces unrelated supplied retrieval hits; no broad search or citation traversal.'), indent=2))\n    Path('reference_consultation.md').write_text('\\n\\n'.join(\n        f'## {record[\"citation_key\"]}: {record[\"title\"]}\\n{record[\"source_url\"]}\\n\\n{record[\"abstract\"] or \"No abstract returned. Metadata only; use only for bibliographic context.\"}'\n        for _, record in results))\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
