{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sha256sum paper_data.json derived_results.json figure_manifest.json figures/*.pdf > /tmp/paper-citation-enrichment/protected-artifact-hashes.txt; date -u '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ'; sha256sum /tmp/paper-citation-enrichment/baker-2001.pdf /tmp/paper-citation-enrichment/baker-eric.html /tmp/paper-citation-enrichment/leaderboard-space.html; apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: paper.tex\n@@\n-The nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model. Source abilities are fixed at \"'$'\"\\\\theta_i=\\\\operatorname{logit}[(\\\\sum_jX_{ij}+0.5)/(N+1)]\"'$. Item difficulties $d_j$ are fitted by group-weighted Bernoulli likelihood with a quadratic penalty. Write $r_{ij}='\"\\\\sigmoid(\\\\theta_i-d_j)\"'$ and\n+The nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch response model, a logistic ability--difficulty family '\"\\\\citep[Chap.~2]{Baker2001}. Our custom fit fixes source abilities at \"'$'\"\\\\theta_i=\\\\operatorname{logit}[(\\\\sum_jX_{ij}+0.5)/(N+1)]\"'$. Item difficulties $d_j$ are fitted by group-weighted Bernoulli likelihood with a quadratic penalty. Write $r_{ij}='\"\\\\sigmoid(\\\\theta_i-d_j)\"'$ and\n@@\n-The final assessment contains four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math) and six HELM-derived cases (Commonsense, GSM, LegalBench, MATH, MedQA, and MMLU). We retain these staged case names rather than asserting identity with every similarly named public benchmark. Each case uses a fixed binary item universe. The question is prediction for held-out models on that universe, not generalization to newly sampled questions or unseen benchmark distributions.\n+The final assessment contains four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math) and six HELM-derived cases (Commonsense, GSM, LegalBench, MATH, MedQA, and MMLU). The upstream collections are documented by '\"\\\\citet{OpenLLMLeaderboard} and \\\\citet{Liang2023}, respectively; our case identities and filtering come from the supplied data manifest. We retain these staged case names rather than asserting identity with every similarly named public benchmark. Each case uses a fixed binary item universe. The question is prediction for held-out models on that universe, not generalization to newly sampled questions or unseen benchmark distributions.\n@@\n-The anchor reference clusters centered, normalized item response vectors and estimates scores from representatives weighted by assigned cluster sizes. The IRT reference fits a penalized two-parameter logistic response model, chooses items by fitted information near zero ability, and infers target ability from queried responses. These references use the same query budgets, but they are local comparators, not official reproductions of Anchor Points or tinyBenchmarks. A source-mean reference predicts the average source score without target responses. Extended fitting details are in the supplement.\n+The anchor reference clusters centered, normalized item response vectors and estimates scores from representatives weighted by assigned cluster sizes. The IRT reference fits a penalized two-parameter logistic response model, chooses items by fitted information near zero ability, and infers target ability from queried responses; the response family and item-information definition follow standard IRT terminology \\\\citep[Chaps.~2,6]{Baker2001}. These references use the same query budgets, but they are local comparators, not official reproductions of Anchor Points or tinyBenchmarks. A source-mean reference predicts the average source score without target responses. Extended fitting details are in the supplement.\n@@\n-The IRT reference models logits as \"'$'\"\\\\theta_i\\\\exp(\\\\eta_j)-d_j\"'$. Its objective is summed Bernoulli negative log likelihood plus penalties $0.5'\"\\\\|\\\\theta\\\\|\"'^2+0.05'\"\\\\|\\\\eta\\\\|\"'^2+0.005'\"\\\\|d\\\\|\"'^2$. L-BFGS-B uses bounded parameters and at most 40 iterations. Item order is the decreasing fitted information $a_j''^2 p_j(1-p_j)$ at zero ability, where $a_j='\"\\\\exp(\\\\eta_j)\"'$. Target abilities use up to 30 regularized Newton updates on queried responses; the reference score averages fitted response probabilities over the entire item universe. Finite-objective checks do not prove solver optimality. No exact reproduction of a published IRT benchmark-compression package is claimed.\n+The IRT reference models logits as $'\"\\\\theta_i\\\\exp(\\\\eta_j)-d_j\"'$. Its objective is summed Bernoulli negative log likelihood plus penalties $0.5'\"\\\\|\\\\theta\\\\|\"'^2+0.05'\"\\\\|\\\\eta\\\\|\"'^2+0.005'\"\\\\|d\\\\|\"'^2$. L-BFGS-B uses bounded parameters and at most 40 iterations. Item order is the decreasing fitted information $a_j''^2 p_j(1-p_j)$ at zero ability, where $a_j='\"\\\\exp(\\\\eta_j)\"'$ '\"\\\\citep[Chap.~6]{Baker2001}. Target abilities use up to 30 regularized Newton updates on queried responses; the reference score averages fitted response probabilities over the entire item universe. Finite-objective checks do not prove solver optimality. No exact reproduction of a published IRT benchmark-compression package is claimed.\n*** Update File: references.bib\n@@\n @misc{Brier1950,\n@@\n  doi = {10.1175/1520-0493(1950)078<0001:VOFEIT>2.0.CO;2}\n }\n+\n+@book{Baker2001,\n+ title = {The Basics of Item Response Theory},\n+ author = {Baker, Frank B.},\n+ year = {2001},\n+ edition = {Second},\n+ publisher = {ERIC Clearinghouse on Assessment and Evaluation},\n+ address = {College Park, MD},\n+ isbn = {1-886047-03-0},\n+ url = {https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED458219.pdf}\n+}\n+\n+@misc{OpenLLMLeaderboard,\n+ title = {Open {LLM} Leaderboard},\n+ author = {{Hugging Face}},\n+ year = {n.d.},\n+ howpublished = {Hugging Face Space},\n+ url = {https://huggingface.co/spaces/open-llm-leaderboard/open_llm_leaderboard},\n+ note = {Living collection documentation; accessed November 13, 2026}\n+}\n*** Update File: claims_grounding.json\n@@\n-      \\\"grounding_note\\\": \\\"Permitted exact-DOI metadata and abstracts were inspected; see reference_consultation.md. No citation-graph traversal or prohibited-source identification was used.\\\"\n+      \\\"grounding_note\\\": \\\"Permitted exact-DOI metadata and abstracts were inspected; see reference_consultation.md. No citation-graph traversal or prohibited-source identification was used.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM are the named upstream evaluation collections. Their documentation provides context, not evidence for staged case identities, counts, filtering or snapshot equivalence.\\\",\n+      \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"ref.huggingface.open_llm_leaderboard\\\", \\\"ref.openalex.W4320003957\\\", \\\"final.evidence.episodes\\\"],\n+      \\\"citation_keys\\\": [\\\"OpenLLMLeaderboard\\\", \\\"Liang2023\\\"],\n+      \\\"evidence_refs\\\": [\\\"@evidence[[reference_consultation.md#targeted-collection-context]]\\\", \\\"@evidence[[source_evidence/final_data_manifest.json]]\\\"]\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The Rasch response family has logistic ability-minus-difficulty form with common unit discrimination; the 2PL family permits item-specific discrimination and has item information a\"'^2 p(1-p). These foundations do not certify the custom fitting procedure or establish a profile-component benefit.\",\n+      \"source_ids\": [\"ref.eric.ED458219\"],\n+      \"citation_keys\": [\"Baker2001\"],\n+      \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence[[reference_consultation.md#targeted-irt-foundations]]\"],\n+      \"grounding_note\": \"Visually inspected Baker (2001), printed pages 22, 25 and 111 (PDF pages 31, 34 and 120), including equations 2-1, 2-2 and 6-3. Implementation claims remain grounded separately in readable evaluated source.\"\n     }\n*** End Patch\nPATCH'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
