{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"/tmp/paper-citation-enrichment/apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: reference_consultation.md\n@@\n ## Brier1950: VERIFICATION OF FORECASTS EXPRESSED IN TERMS OF PROBABILITY\n https://api.openalex.org/works/https://doi.org/10.1175/1520-0493(1950)078<0001:VOFEIT>2.0.CO;2\n \n-No abstract returned. Metadata only; use only for bibliographic context.\n+No abstract returned. Metadata only; use only for bibliographic context.\n+\n+## Targeted IRT Foundations\n+\n+Citation key: \"'`Baker2001`. Frank B. Baker, *The Basics of Item Response Theory*, second edition, ERIC Clearinghouse on Assessment and Evaluation, 2001. Author, edition, publisher, year and ISBN were checked against the ERIC record and the scanned copyright page. The exact-source retrieval used https://eric.ed.gov/?id=ED458219 and https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED458219.pdf, not a broad search or a citation graph. The inspected PDF SHA-256 is `d310bb6600fc70597b7d7c96280923408ad108e6517d53fdb3bb66fc0d929627`.\n+\n+The scanned PDF has no extractable text; selected pages were rendered and visually read. Printed page 22 (PDF page 31), equation 2-1, defines the 2PL response as the logistic of discrimination times ability minus item difficulty. Printed page 25 (PDF page 34), equation 2-2, fixes discrimination at one for all items in the Rasch model. Printed page 111 (PDF page 120), equation 6-3, gives 2PL item information as squared discrimination times response probability times its complement. These definitions support both targeted IRT paragraphs and the supplementary information formula. The source'\"'s difficulty parameterization is discrimination times ability-minus-difficulty; the evaluated comparator instead uses a freely fitted logit intercept, as stated in the manuscript. The citation does not establish the correctness or empirical benefit of that custom fit. No additional IRT source was needed. Also inspected the response-model introduction and contents; no bibliography links were traversed.\n+\n+@evidence[[references_provenance.json#/references/25]]\n+\n+## Targeted Collection Context\n+\n+Citation key: \"'`OpenLLMLeaderboard`. Consulted the exact permitted official Space at https://huggingface.co/spaces/open-llm-leaderboard/open_llm_leaderboard on November 13, 2026, plus its official README. The Space title identifies Open LLM Leaderboard; its metadata description names IFEval, BBH, MATH, GPQA, MUSR and MMLU-PRO. The README describes comparing open language models in an open, reproducible interface. These are collection-context facts only. The manuscript'\"'s staged identities, counts, binary normalization, filtering and snapshot remain supported by the supplied manifest and recorded evaluation, not by the living webpage. No publication date was established for the current documentation, so the reference is explicitly undated. Reuse \"'`Liang2023` for HELM context.\n+\n+@evidence[[references_provenance.json#/references/26]]\n+\n+The two exact blog destinations `https://huggingface.co/blog/leaderboard-v2` and `https://huggingface.co/blog/open-llm-leaderboard-v2` returned HTTP 404 and were not used as references. No broad query, community discussion, related-paper recommendation or citation graph was read in this enrichment pass. No prohibited source was identified or encountered. The reference additions do not modify the failed qualification or any measured result.\n*** Update File: references.bib.prov.json\n@@\n-  \"notes\": \"Exact-DOI metadata used for bibliography.\",\n+  \"notes\": \"Base bibliography from exact-DOI metadata. Baker2001 and OpenLLMLeaderboard were added in a targeted manual pass using inspected official full text/collection documentation and the appended records in references_provenance.json. The base retrieval script alone does not regenerate these two curated additions.\",\n*** Update File: reference_consultation.md.prov.j